/* TNM World Wrestling Report: WWF (World Wrestling Federation).
   Everything the site shows for this promotion lives in this file. */

P.wwf = {"short": "WWF", "name": "World Wrestling Federation", "home": "Stamford, CT", "c": "var(--wwf)", "status": "PERFECT ESCAPES SATO AS LOD RETAIN IN TORONTO. At Maple Leaf Gardens 7/21 (14,200, $255,600), Heenan grabbed SATO's foot and Mr. Perfect kept the IC Title with the Perfect-Plex (****1/4); the Mountie made Shane Douglas submit in the other ****1/4. LOD beat Power & Glory in their first defense, but Animal hurt his elbow. Hogan beat the Warlord with Sid watching from the corner. On Challenge that morning, Rick Martel made Jimmy Snuka submit and warned SATO.", "stat": ["$3,397,420", "July gates, 14 shows, plus $3.1M in PPV"], "titles": [["WWF Championship", "Hulk Hogan", "Retained vs. the Warlord in Toronto 7/21 (Wippleman interfered; Sid Justice in the Warlord's corner). Sid wants him at SummerSlam."], ["Intercontinental", "Mr. Perfect", "Retained vs. SATO in Toronto 7/21 (****1/4) when Heenan grabbed SATO's foot. Heenan's open challenge for SummerSlam still stands."], ["World Tag Team", "Legion of Doom", "Hawk & Animal beat the Nasty Boys in a Death Match, 7 falls to 4, 55:59, at the Spectrum 7/20. First defense: beat Power & Glory in Toronto 7/21 (Animal hurt his elbow). The Nasty Boys want a rematch."], ["Million Dollar Championship", "Ted DiBiase", "DiBiase beat Virgil at WrestleFest, but Virgil ran off with the belt. IRS beat Virgil by DQ in Dayton 7/19 when Piper ran in; Virgil still has the belt."]], "factions": [["Jimmy Hart", "IRS, Ted DiBiase, the Mountie and Earthquake. Fired Dino Bravo 7/18."], ["Bobby Heenan", "Mr. Perfect, Haku and the Barbarian."], ["Harvey Wippleman", "Sid Justice and Warlord."], ["Mr. Fuji", "The Berzerker and the Orient Express."], ["Paul Bearer", "The Undertaker."], ["Gen. Adnan", "Sgt. Slaughter."], ["Coach", "The Beverly Brothers."], ["Miss Elizabeth", "Randy Savage, who is retired and working non-wrestling segments."]], "stories": [["Perfect Escapes SATO", "SATO had Mr. Perfect beaten in Toronto 7/21 until Heenan grabbed his foot on the top rope; Perfect retained with the Perfect-Plex in 14:03, ****1/4.", "wwf-toronto-perfect-sato-0721"], ["Heenan Chooses", "Backstage in Toronto, Heenan told Haku to his face he'd be with Perfect instead of at ringside. Haku walked off and lost to Jake Roberts alone.", "wwf-heenan-chooses-0721"], ["Martel Puts SATO on Notice", "Rick Martel made Jimmy Snuka submit to the Boston crab on Challenge 7/21 after a thumb to the eye, then told SATO that excitement fades but arrogance is forever.", "wwf-challenge-ep1-martel-0721"], ["LOD Take the Gold", "The Legion of Doom demanded a Death Match for the tag titles and beat the Nasty Boys seven falls to four in 55:59 at the Spectrum 7/20. Hawk took all seven falls, two by DQ for Jimmy Hart's megaphone; Knobbs took all four for the Nastys.", "wwf-lod-champions-0720"], ["Sid Won't Tag", "Hogan & Duggan beat Slaughter & Sid at the Spectrum 7/20, and Sid never tagged in. He walked out with Wippleman and stared Hogan down from the aisle. Wippleman, who now manages the Warlord too, wants Hogan-Sid at SummerSlam.", "wwf-sid-wont-tag-0720"], ["Jake and the Urn", "Jake Roberts beat the Berzerker 7/20, then stopped on the ramp, looked at Paul Bearer and the urn, and nodded.", "wwf-jake-and-the-urn-0720"], ["Martel Steals One", "Rick Martel and SATO had the match of the night in Dayton 7/19 (****1/4), and Martel won it with his feet on the ropes. SATO argued all the way to the back."], ["Bravo Walks Alone", "Jimmy Hart fired Dino Bravo after his loss to Tito Santana in Richfield 7/18, and Bravo press slammed him out of the ring. The next night in Dayton Hart brought Typhoon for revenge. Bravo knocked Hart off the apron and beat Typhoon with a side suplex, and left to a real cheer.", "wwf-bravo-walks-alone-0719"], ["Sid Strikes", "Sid Justice and Harvey Wippleman powerbombed Hulk Hogan and Jim Duggan after WrestleFest. In Dayton 7/19 Sid lost to Big Boss Man by DQ when Wippleman grabbed Boss Man's leg, then powerbombed Boss Man and posed with an imaginary title. Hogan vs. Sid at SummerSlam.", "wwf-wrestlefest-recap"], ["Warrior Hurt", "The Ultimate Warrior injured his neck losing to the Undertaker at WrestleFest. Jake Roberts took his time saving him from the casket.", "wwf-warrior-hurt-0714"], ["Rockers Crack", "Marty Jannetty took the pin in the WrestleFest tag title match and Shawn Michaels shoved him afterward. In Richfield 7/18 they fought Demolition to a double countout (***1/4), and Michaels left Jannetty alone in the ring. In Dayton 7/19 Michaels beat Paul Roma on his own and celebrated alone. Jannetty re-signed with the company after the show.", "wwf-jannetty-resigns-0719"], ["Virgil Takes the Belt", "Virgil stole the Million Dollar Belt after losing to DiBiase. Roddy Piper ran off DiBiase and IRS. In Richfield 7/18, IRS attacked Virgil after his match and took the belt up the ramp; Piper knocked him down and gave it back. In Dayton 7/19 IRS went for Virgil with the briefcase; Piper stopped him in front of the referee, Virgil was disqualified, and he kept the belt.", "wwf-virgil-takes-the-belt-0719"], ["Tito Steals the Show", "Tito Santana beat the Mountie (***3/4) at WrestleFest and is next in line for Mr. Perfect.", "wwf-tito-steals-the-show-0714"], ["Crush vs. Tenryu", "Crush pinned Tenryu in Milwaukee, then pulled him out of the ring at WrestleFest so Demolition could beat Tenryu & Kitao. They kept brawling after the bell. A Crush vs. Tenryu singles match is coming to Superstars.", "wwf-crush-vs-tenryu-0714"], ["Piper's Back", "Rowdy Roddy Piper returned in Milwaukee 7/13 as Sid Justice's surprise opponent. They brawled to a double countout at 16:05 and kept fighting after the bell, the night before WrestleFest.", "wwf-pipers-back"], ["Sid Is Watching", "Sid saved Hogan at the Vancouver taping and stared at the belt. Wippleman jawed at Hogan in Calgary. In Saskatoon, Sid sat at ringside for the whole Hogan–Warlord main event."], ["The Edmonton Injuries", "Earthquake (neck), Greg Valentine (neck) and Bushwhacker Butch (elbow) all went down on 7/9. Typhoon replaces Earthquake against Jake at WrestleFest."], ["Jake Hesitates", "Jake Roberts no-showed MSG, beat Earthquake twice, and then, in Edmonton, waited a beat too long before saving the Warrior."], ["Nastys Survive", "The Nasty Boys kept the tag titles on a count-out against LOD in Saskatoon. Paul Ellering showed up in LOD's corner. LOD hit the Doomsday Device on Knobbs after."], ["Owen Steals the Show", "Owen Hart beat Rick Martel in Calgary (***1/2) on the best-rated WWF card of July. 13,000 paid $260,000."], ["Garden Gold, Road Rust", "MSG drew $543,200 for a *1/2 card. Warrior beat the Undertaker three nights running, and the ratings kept falling."], ["Cuffed", "The Mountie handcuffed Big Boss Man to the ropes in Calgary. Boss Man is at ringside for Mountie vs. Tito at WrestleFest."], ["Virgil Strikes Back", "Virgil cut IRS off in Calgary and pinned him in Edmonton. He gets DiBiase one on one at WrestleFest."], ["The SWS Experiment", "Tenryu & Kitao had the co-match of the night in Edmonton (***1/4). SATO went *** in Calgary, then lost by DQ in 42 seconds in Saskatoon."], ["The Bulldog's Two Masters", "Davey Boy Smith is finishing the All Japan tour and won't be back until about 7/29. The office isn't happy."], ["Steamboat Down", "Ricky Steamboat tore his right ACL against Skinner in Des Moines on 7/5. On the WrestleFest Barber Shop he vowed to come back, and when Skinner mocked him from the stage, Steamboat stopped Beefcake from going after him: \"He's mine.\""]], "desk": ["Toronto 7/21: 14,200 at $18 = $255,600, merch $56,800. Card **. MOTN: Perfect vs. SATO and Mountie vs. Douglas, ****1/4. Hogan-Warlord -*1/2.", "Road Warrior Animal hurt his elbow in Toronto (about one card). LOD are booked in Chicago 7/27.", "Challenge Ep. 1 aired 7/21 (**1/2): Martel d. Snuka **1/2. Bravo's first promo on his own and Piper's Barber Shop.", "A Hogan interview taped in Toronto is held for All American Wrestling 7/28.", "Philadelphia 7/20: 15,800 at $18 = $284,400, merch $63,200. Card **1/2. MOTN: Snuka vs. Mountie and Tito vs. Martel, ****1/4. LOD won the tag titles.", "Superstars Ep. 2 aired 7/20 (***): Tito d. Warlord ***, Perfect d. Shane Douglas **3/4. Harvey Wippleman now manages the Warlord as well as Sid.", "Dayton 7/19: 4,000 at $12 = $48,000, merch $10,000. Card *1/2. MOTN: Martel vs. SATO, ****1/4. WCW drew $63,000 at the Nutter Center in Dayton 7/17.", "Marty Jannetty re-signed after Dayton 7/19: 32-card deal (×10 = 320 cards), 8% raise from Philadelphia 7/20. No rush on the Rockers split now.", "Bravo vs. Typhoon went -****3/4. Don't run it again; give Bravo a better worker.", "Martel's cheating win over SATO sets up the spray angle on Challenge.", "Nine July house shows still need cards: Philadelphia 7/20, Toronto 7/21, Amarillo 7/25, San Francisco 7/26, Chicago 7/27, Hartford 7/28, Worcester 7/29, Portland, ME 7/30, Scranton 7/31.", "TV this week: Superstars Ep 2 (7/20), Challenge Ep 1 (7/21), Prime Time from Richfield (7/22), Superstars Ep 3 (7/27), Challenge Ep 2 (7/28).", "Superstars Ep 2: the WrestleFest update insert needs rewriting for the real results, and the Natural Disasters reunion needs a rethink with Earthquake out.", "SummerSlam '91: Sunday 8/25, Madison Square Garden, head to head with WCW at the Omni. Card announcements start on Superstars 8/3.", "Injured: Ultimate Warrior (neck, WrestleFest, about 40 shows, past SummerSlam), Earthquake (neck, 50 shows), Greg Valentine (neck, back mid/late August). Bushwhacker Butch back in Toronto 7/21.", "Steamboat (torn right ACL): the roster export says 12 more cards; the earlier estimate was about 10 months. Needs a ruling.", "Davey Boy Smith is in All Japan through Budokan 7/27. Back at Worcester 7/29.", "Hawk's run-in on Sgt. Slaughter in Regina is still loose. LOD vs. Slaughter is the SummerSlam option while Earthquake is out.", "Paul Ellering appeared with LOD in Saskatoon, but he's a USWA manager from Dallas 7/20. Explain it or drop it.", "Keep out of singles: Warlord, Beefcake, Kitao, Crush. Sid Justice's loop matches have rated badly; keep his matches short until SummerSlam."], "roster": [["Ax", "Demolition"], ["Beau Beverly", "Beverly Brothers · w/ Coach"], ["Big Boss Man", "vs. the Mountie"], ["Blake Beverly", "Beverly Brothers · w/ Coach"], ["Brian Knobbs", "Lost the tag titles to LOD in the Death Match, Philadelphia 7/20"], ["Brooklyn Brawler", "Debuted 7/13 (draw with Virgil)"], ["Brutus Beefcake", ""], ["Bushwhacker Butch", "Back from his elbow injury, Toronto 7/21"], ["Bushwhacker Luke", "Bushwhackers"], ["Crush", "Demolition · ringside for Ax & Smash at WrestleFest"], ["Davey Boy Smith", "Unavailable until ~7/29"], ["Dino Bravo", "Fired by Jimmy Hart 7/18 · babyface, beat Typhoon 7/19"], ["Earthquake", "INJ:Neck (7/9), out 50 shows, back after SummerSlam · w/ Jimmy Hart"], ["Genichiro Tenryu", "SWS"], ["Greg Valentine", "INJ:Neck (7/9), out 30 shows, back mid/late August"], ["Haku", "w/ Heenan"], ["Hercules", "Power & Glory"], ["Hulk Hogan", "WWF Champion"], ["Irwin R. Schyster", "Money Inc. · w/ Jimmy Hart"], ["Jake Roberts", "Hesitated before saving Warrior 7/9"], ["Jerry Sags", "Lost the tag titles to LOD in the Death Match, Philadelphia 7/20"], ["Jim Duggan", ""], ["Jimmy Snuka", ""], ["Kato", "Orient Express · w/ Fuji"], ["Koji Kitao", "SWS"], ["Koko B. Ware", "High Energy"], ["Marty Jannetty", "Rockers · Re-signed 7/19: 32 cards (×10 = 320), 8% raise from Philadelphia 7/20"], ["Mr. Perfect", "Intercontinental Champion · w/ Heenan"], ["Owen Hart", "High Energy · IC challenger at WrestleFest"], ["Paul Roma", "Power & Glory"], ["Randy Savage", "Retired · w/ Elizabeth · Superstars commentary"], ["Rick Martel", ""], ["Roddy Piper", "Saved Virgil at WrestleFest · vs. IRS"], ["Road Warrior Animal", "INJ:Elbow (Toronto 7/21), out ~1 card · WWF Tag Team Champion"], ["Road Warrior Hawk", "WWF TAG TEAM CHAMPION (Death Match vs. Nasty Boys, Philadelphia 7/20)"], ["SATO", "SWS"], ["Sgt. Slaughter", "w/ Gen. Adnan"], ["Shane Douglas", ""], ["Shawn Michaels", "Rockers · shoved Jannetty after WrestleFest"], ["Sid Justice", "w/ Harvey Wippleman · ambushed Hogan at WrestleFest"], ["Skinner", ""], ["Smash", "Demolition"], ["Tanaka", "Orient Express · w/ Fuji"], ["Ted DiBiase", "Money Inc. · w/ Jimmy Hart"], ["The Barbarian", "w/ Heenan"], ["The Berzerker", "w/ Fuji"], ["The Mountie", "w/ Jimmy Hart"], ["The Ultimate Warrior", "INJ:Neck (WrestleFest 7/14), out ~40 shows"], ["The Undertaker", "w/ Paul Bearer"], ["Tito Santana", "Next in line for the IC title"], ["Typhoon", "Natural Disasters · replaces Earthquake at WrestleFest"], ["Virgil", "Has the Million Dollar Belt · Piper took it back from IRS 7/18 · vs. DiBiase at SummerSlam"], ["Warlord", "w/ Harvey Wippleman"], ["Ricky Steamboat", "INJ:Torn right ACL (7/5). Roster export: 12 more cards"], ["Jimmy Hart", "Manager"], ["Bobby Heenan", "Manager"], ["Harvey Wippleman", "Manager: Sid Justice and the Warlord"], ["Mr. Fuji", "Manager"], ["Paul Bearer", "Manager"], ["Gen. Adnan", "Manager"], ["Coach", "Manager"], ["Miss Elizabeth", "Manager"], ["Colonel Mustafa", "Signed (20 cards)"], ["Jerry Simmons", "Enhancement (29 cards)"]], "departures": [["Big Bubba Rogers", "No longer with the WWF (7/10)."]], "asOf": "1991-07-21"};

E.push(...[{"d":"1991-07-01","p":"wwf","link":"WWF_NEW_","city":"New York, NY","venue":"Madison Square Garden","type":"House show, taped for Prime Time","gate":"15,520 · $543,200","rate":"*1/2","big":1,"m":[["Dino Bravo vs. Shane Douglas","Bravo (side suplex)","*1/4"],["Ricky Steamboat vs. Hercules, no countout","Steamboat (diving crossbody)","****1/4"],["The Berzerker vs. Jimmy Snuka","Berzerker (guillotine drop)","3/4*"],["Tag Titles: Nasty Boys vs. Rockers","Nastys retain by DQ. Jimmy Hart involved","**1/4"],["Warlord vs. Greg Valentine","Warlord (Full Nelson submission)","***1/4"],["Jim Duggan vs. Earthquake","Duggan (three point stance). Jake Roberts no-showed","1/4*"],["Haku & Barbarian vs. Orient Express","Barbarian by DQ. Heenan and Fuji interfere","*1/4"],["Casket Match: Ultimate Warrior vs. The Undertaker","Warrior. Paul Bearer interferes","*"]],"n":["Randy Savage on the Barbershop.","Top merch: Warrior, Savage, Undertaker."],"reads":[["Garden Gold, Road Rust","wwf-garden-gold"]]},{"d":"1991-07-05","p":"wwf","link":"WWF_DES_","city":"Des Moines, IA","venue":"Veterans Memorial Auditorium","type":"House show","gate":"4,500 · $90,000","rate":"1/4*","m":[["Tito Santana vs. The Berzerker","Santana (El Paseo del Muerte)","**"],["IC Title: Mr. Perfect vs. Davey Boy Smith","Perfect retains (Perfect Plex)","***1/2"],["Power & Glory vs. Bushwhackers","Roma pins Butch in 36 seconds","DUD"],["Jake Roberts vs. Earthquake","Jake (DDT)","*1/4"],["Skinner vs. Ricky Steamboat","Skinner (back suplex). Steamboat tears his right ACL","*3/4"],["Warlord vs. Virgil","Warlord, 24:49","-*1/2"],["Ultimate Warrior vs. The Undertaker","Warrior by DQ (Bearer's urn). Taker chokes Warrior after","-1/4*"]],"n":["Ricky Steamboat tears his right ACL."],"reads":[["Garden Gold, Road Rust","wwf-garden-gold"]]},{"d":"1991-07-06","p":"wwf","link":"WWF_ANAH","city":"Anaheim, CA","venue":"Convention Center","type":"House show","gate":"5,279 · $105,580","rate":"1/2*","m":[["Money Inc. vs. Virgil & Shane Douglas","DiBiase by DQ. British Bulldog interferes","1/4*"],["Big Boss Man vs. Dino Bravo","Boss Man (powerslam). Jimmy Hart interferes","**"],["The Mountie vs. Tito Santana","Mountie (Carotid Control). Hart interferes","****"],["Islanders (Haku & Tama) vs. Bushwhackers","Haku (savate kick). Coliseum Video","**1/2"],["Jake Roberts vs. Earthquake","Jake (DDT). Damien scatters the Hart camp","*"],["Crush vs. Brutus Beefcake","Crush by countout","-***1/4"],["Bodybag Match: Ultimate Warrior vs. The Undertaker","Warrior bags Taker",""]],"reads":[["Garden Gold, Road Rust","wwf-garden-gold"]]},{"d":"1991-07-07","p":"wwf","city":"Vancouver, BC","venue":"BC Place","type":"Superstars taping (4 episodes)","m":[["Ep 1 (airs 7/13): Hulk Hogan vs. local talent","Hogan (legdrop). Slaughter & Adnan attack; Sid Justice saves, then stares at the belt",""],["Ep 1: Mean Gene with Hogan","Hogan names Jim Duggan his WrestleFest enforcer",""],["Ep 1: Beverly Brothers (w/ Coach) vs. local team","Beverlys. High Energy make the save; WrestleFest match made",""],["Ep 1: Perfect & Heenan with Mean Gene","Greg Valentine takes the IC Title shot",""],["Eps 2–4 (7/20, 7/27, 8/3)","SummerSlam seeds: Natural Disasters reunite, Jailhouse challenge, Jake's \"training\" vignettes, Savage–Elizabeth wedding",""]],"n":["Superstars airs Saturdays at 10 AM in syndication.","Vince McMahon and Randy Savage call the show."]},{"d":"1991-07-08","p":"wwf","city":"Calgary, AB","venue":"Olympic Saddledome","type":"House show","gate":"13,000 · $260,000","rate":"**","m":[["SATO vs. Shane Douglas","SATO (layback suplex)","***"],["Rockers vs. Orient Express","Jannetty pins Kato (Showstopper)","**1/4"],["The Undertaker vs. Jimmy Snuka","Undertaker (Tombstone)","*1/4"],["Big Boss Man vs. Earthquake","Boss Man by DQ (Hart). The Mountie handcuffs Boss Man after","3/4*"],["LOD vs. Money Inc.","Animal pins IRS. Virgil cuts IRS off at ringside","***"],["Owen Hart vs. Rick Martel","Owen (missile dropkick). Match of the night","***1/2"],["IC Title: Mr. Perfect vs. Tito Santana","Perfect retains. Heenan interferes. 24:16","**1/4"],["Hogan & Duggan vs. Slaughter & Warlord","Hogan pins Warlord. Wippleman jaws at Hogan after","3/4*"]],"n":["Best-rated WWF card of July."],"link":"WWF_CALG","reads":[["Owen Steals the Show","wwf-owen-steals-calgary"],["Observer: Backstage Notes: The Canadian Swing","wwf-observer-notes-canada"]]},{"d":"1991-07-09","p":"wwf","city":"Edmonton, AB","venue":"Northlands Coliseum","type":"House show","gate":"10,050 · $201,000","rate":"*1/2","m":[["Koko B. Ware vs. Skinner","Koko (Ghostbuster)","**1/4"],["Bushwhackers vs. Orient Express","Luke pins Kato (battering ram). Butch hurts elbow","**3/4"],["Tenryu & Kitao vs. Power & Glory","Tenryu pins Roma (powerbomb)","***1/4"],["Sid Justice vs. Brutus Beefcake","Sid (powerbomb)","-***1/4"],["Virgil & Greg Valentine vs. Money Inc.","Virgil pins IRS (roll-up). Valentine hurts neck","**3/4"],["Rockers vs. Beverly Brothers","Michaels pins Beau. Coach interferes","***1/4"],["Mr. Perfect vs. Owen Hart (non-title)","Perfect (neckbreaker). Heenan interferes","**3/4"],["Warrior & Jake Roberts vs. Undertaker & Earthquake","Warrior pins Earthquake (splash). Earthquake hurts neck. Jake hesitates before the save","1/4*"]],"n":["Three injuries: Earthquake (~5 cards), Valentine (~3), Butch (~1).","Earthquake out of WrestleFest; Typhoon takes his match with Jake."],"link":"WWF_EDMO","reads":[["The Edmonton Injuries","wwf-edmonton-injuries"],["Observer: Backstage Notes: The Canadian Swing","wwf-observer-notes-canada"]]},{"d":"1991-07-10","p":"wwf","city":"Saskatoon, SK","venue":"Saskatchewan Place","type":"House show","gate":"6,500 · $110,500","rate":"*1/2","m":[["Skinner vs. SATO","Skinner by DQ, 0:42","*"],["High Energy vs. Orient Express","Koko pins Tanaka (Ghostbuster)","***"],["Shane Douglas vs. The Berzerker","Douglas (small package). Fuji interferes","*3/4"],["Big Boss Man vs. Dino Bravo","Boss Man (Boss Man slam). Hart interferes","1/4*"],["Tito Santana vs. Rick Martel","Tito (flying forearm)","***1/4"],["Sid Justice vs. Hercules","Sid (powerbomb). Wippleman interferes","**"],["The Undertaker vs. Jim Duggan","Undertaker (Tombstone)","**"],["Tag Titles: Nasty Boys vs. LOD","Nastys retain by count-out. Ellering with LOD. Doomsday Device on Knobbs after","*1/2"],["Hulk Hogan vs. Warlord","Hogan (legdrop). Sid at ringside, staring at the belt","DUD"]],"n":["Big Bubba Rogers is gone from the WWF."],"link":"WWF_SASK","reads":[["Nastys Survive","wwf-nastys-survive"],["Observer: Backstage Notes: The Canadian Swing","wwf-observer-notes-canada"]]},{"d":"1991-07-11","p":"wwf","city":"Regina, SK","venue":"Agridome","type":"House show","gate":"4,200 · $63,000","rate":"*1/4","m":[["Beverly Brothers (w/ Coach) vs. Bushwhacker Luke & Crush","Beau pins Luke (flying axhandle). Coach interferes","**1/2"],["SATO vs. Shane Douglas","SATO (belly-to-back superplex). Match of the night","***"],["Dino Bravo vs. Koko B. Ware","Bravo (side suplex)","*"],["Genichiro Tenryu vs. Haku","Tenryu (powerbomb)","*3/4"],["Big Boss Man vs. Skinner","Boss Man (Boss Man slam)","**3/4"],["Rockers vs. Money Inc.","Michaels pins IRS (side suplex)","**3/4"],["Jim Duggan vs. Sid Justice","Duggan by DQ (Wippleman interferes)","-**"],["Ultimate Warrior vs. Sgt. Slaughter","Slaughter by DQ. Adnan, then Hawk interfere","*3/4"]],"n":["Jimmy Snuka no-showed; Crush replaced him.","Smallest crowd and gate of July.","Hawk's run-in on Slaughter starts an unplanned LOD thread."],"link":"WWF_REGI","reads":[["The Canadian Swing Wraps","wwf-canadian-swing-wraps"],["Observer report","wwf-observer-regina-0711"]]},{"d":"1991-07-12","p":"wwf","city":"Winnipeg, MB","venue":"Winnipeg Arena","type":"House show","gate":"10,100 · $202,000","rate":"**1/4","m":[["High Energy vs. Power & Glory","Owen pins Roma (Rocket Launcher). Match of the night","***1/2"],["Koji Kitao vs. Crush","Kitao (big boot)","-1/4*"],["Jimmy Snuka vs. The Berzerker","Snuka (Superfly splash). Fuji interferes","3/4*"],["IRS vs. Virgil","Virgil passes out in the Penalty. Hart interferes","**1/4"],["Typhoon vs. Shane Douglas","Typhoon (big splash)","*"],["Jake Roberts vs. Rick Martel","Jake (DDT)","**"],["The Undertaker vs. Hercules","Undertaker (Tombstone)","**1/2"],["Tito Santana & Jim Duggan vs. Mr. Perfect & Barbarian","Tito pins Barbarian. Perfect walks out; Heenan interferes","***"],["Hulk Hogan & Big Boss Man vs. Sgt. Slaughter & The Mountie","Boss Man pins the Mountie (Boss Man slam). Hart interferes","**3/4"]],"n":["Best card of the tour. Every planned finish went as booked.","Hogan's best rating of July."],"link":"WWF_WINN","reads":[["The Canadian Swing Wraps","wwf-canadian-swing-wraps"],["Observer report","wwf-observer-winnipeg-0712"],["From the Road: Owen Hart","wwf-diary-owen-0712"]]},{"d":"1991-07-13","p":"wwf","city":"Milwaukee, WI","venue":"Bradley Center","type":"House show","gate":"7,000 · $105,000","rate":"*","m":[["Brooklyn Brawler vs. Virgil","Time-limit draw, 20:00. Wippleman interferes against Virgil. Brawler's debut","*3/4"],["Snuka, Douglas & Beefcake vs. Martel, Bravo & The Berzerker","Snuka pins Berzerker (Superfly Splash)","3/4*"],["Warlord vs. Bushwhacker Luke","Luke passes out in the Full Nelson","1/4*"],["Crush vs. Genichiro Tenryu","Crush (backbreaker)","*1/2"],["Koko B. Ware vs. Skinner","Koko (Ghostbuster). Match of the night","**1/4"],["Sid Justice (w/ Wippleman) vs. Roddy Piper","Double countout, 16:05. Piper's surprise return","DUD"]],"n":["Roddy Piper returns as Sid's surprise opponent.","Light card the night before WrestleFest. $8,000 in merchandise."],"link":"WWF_MILW","reads":[["Piper's Back!","wwf-pipers-back"],["Observer report","wwf-observer-milwaukee-0713"],["From the Road: Roddy Piper","wwf-diary-piper-0713"]]},{"d":"1991-07-13","p":"wwf","city":"Syndication","venue":"","type":"Superstars, Ep 1 (10 AM): WrestleFest go-home","link":"WWF_SU-1"},{"d":"1991-07-14","p":"wwf","city":"St. Louis, MO","venue":"Busch Stadium","type":"WrestleFest '91 (PPV)","big":1,"m":[["Beverly Brothers vs. Koko B. Ware & Bushwhacker Luke","Blake pins Luke (reverse neckbreaker)","***1/4"],["Haku & Barbarian vs. Orient Express","Barbarian by DQ (Fuji)","*1/4"],["Warlord vs. Jimmy Snuka","Warlord (Full Nelson)","-*"],["Legion of Doom vs. Power & Glory","Hawk pins Roma (flying clothesline)","**"],["Demolition vs. Tenryu & Kitao (Crush at ringside)","Smash pins Kitao (backbreaker). Crush and Tenryu brawl","*3/4"],["Ted DiBiase vs. Virgil","DiBiase (back suplex). Virgil steals the belt; Piper saves him","*1/2"],["Jake Roberts vs. Typhoon","Jake (DDT)","-**1/4"],["Tito Santana vs. The Mountie","Tito (flying forearm). Match of the night. Mountie shocks Boss Man","***3/4"],["IC Title: Mr. Perfect vs. Owen Hart","Perfect retains (roll-up). Heenan interferes","***"],["Tag Titles: Nasty Boys vs. Rockers","Nastys retain; Sags pins Jannetty. The Rockers shove each other","***"],["The Undertaker vs. Ultimate Warrior","Undertaker (Tombstone). Warrior hurts his neck; Jake's late save","-1/4*"],["WWF Title: Hulk Hogan vs. Sgt. Slaughter, Duggan special referee","Hogan retains (legdrop). Sid & Wippleman ambush; LOD save","**3/4"]],"n":["125,000 PPV buys ($1,562,500 to the WWF, its half of $3,125,000); $205,428 in merchandise.","The Barber Shop with Brutus Beefcake and Ricky Steamboat; Skinner interrupts.","The Ultimate Warrior hurts his neck.","Head to head with the Great American Bash."],"gate":"51,357 · $1,027,140","rate":"*3/4","link":"WWF_WRES","reads":[["Preview: Hogan vs. Slaughter","wwf-wrestlefest-preview"],["Sid Sends a Message","wwf-wrestlefest-recap"],["Observer report","wwf-observer-wrestlefest-0714"],["From the Road: Marty Jannetty","wwf-diary-jannetty-0714"]]},{"d":"1991-07-18","p":"wwf","link":"WWF_RICH","city":"Richfield, OH","venue":"Richfield Coliseum","type":"All American Wrestling + Prime Time taping","gate":"8,500 · $102,000","rate":"**","m":[["AAW: Brutus Beefcake vs. Jerry Simmons","Beefcake (sleeper), 3:06. Haircut after",""],["AAW: Virgil vs. Dong Phillips","Virgil (cobra clutch), 2:06. IRS takes the belt; Piper gives it back",""],["AAW: SATO vs. Paul Roma","SATO (superplex), 7:03. Co-match of the night","***1/4"],["Prime Time: Tito Santana vs. Dino Bravo (w/ Jimmy Hart)","Tito (flying forearm), 10:45. Hart fires Bravo; Bravo press slams Hart","**"],["Prime Time: Jake Roberts vs. Hercules","Jake (DDT), 8:04","**3/4"],["Prime Time: The Rockers vs. Demolition (Ax & Smash)","Double countout, 16:06. Michaels walks out on Jannetty. Co-match of the night","***1/4"],["Prime Time: Legion of Doom vs. Haku & The Barbarian (w/ Heenan)","LOD (Doomsday Device on Haku), 12:03. Barbarian slow to break the pin","*1/2"],["Dark match: Big Boss Man & Jim Duggan vs. The Mountie & Typhoon (w/ Jimmy Hart)","Boss Man pins Mountie (Boss Man Slam), 8:33. Hart interferes against Duggan","*1/4"]],"n":["No Hogan, no Warrior. WCW drew 5,200 for $52,000 at the Cleveland Convention Center the same night.","Prime Time from this taping airs 7/22 on USA.","8,500 at $12 for $102,000."],"reads":[["Observer report","wwf-observer-richfield-0718"],["From the Road: Shawn Michaels","wwf-diary-michaels-0718"]]},{"d":"1991-07-19","p":"wwf","link":"WWF_DAYT","city":"Dayton, OH","venue":"Hara Arena","type":"House show","ran":1,"gate":"4,000 · $48,000","rate":"*1/2","m":[["Jimmy Snuka vs. Skinner","Snuka (Superfly Splash), 6:11","**"],["High Energy vs. Orient Express (w/ Mr. Fuji)","Owen pins Kato, 10:58. Fuji interferes against Koko","***"],["Rick Martel vs. SATO","Martel (roll-up, feet on the ropes), 10:06. Match of the night","****1/4"],["Dino Bravo vs. Typhoon (w/ Jimmy Hart)","Bravo (side suplex), 7:04, after knocking Hart off the apron","-****3/4"],["Virgil vs. IRS (Million Dollar Belt not at stake)","IRS by DQ, 8:06. Piper attacks IRS; Virgil keeps the belt","3/4*"],["Shawn Michaels vs. Paul Roma","Michaels (suplex), 10:55","***"],["The Undertaker vs. Jim Duggan","Undertaker (Tombstone), 8:06","**1/2"],["Main: Big Boss Man vs. Sid Justice (w/ Wippleman)","Boss Man by DQ, 17:51. Wippleman interferes; Sid powerbombs Boss Man after","*1/4"]],"n":["4,000 at $12 for $48,000, plus $10,000 merchandise. WCW drew 7,000 for $63,000 at the Nutter Center 7/17.","Finish change: Virgil vs. IRS was booked as a Virgil DQ win.","Marty Jannetty re-signed after the show."],"reads":[["Martel Steals One","wwf-martel-steals-one-0719"],["Observer report","wwf-observer-dayton-0719"],["From the Road: Dino Bravo","wwf-diary-bravo-0719"],["Jannetty Re-signs","wwf-jannetty-resigns-0719"]]},{"d": "1991-07-20", "p": "wwf", "link": "WWF_PHIL", "city": "Philadelphia, PA", "venue": "The Spectrum", "type": "Broadcast house show (PRISM)", "ran": 1, "big": 1, "rate": "**1/2", "gate": "15,800 · $284,400", "m": [["SATO vs. Hercules", "SATO (belly-to-back superplex), 8:17", "***1/2"], ["Jimmy Snuka vs. The Mountie (w/ Jimmy Hart)", "Snuka (Superfly Splash), 12:44. Hart interferes; Boss Man runs off the Mountie after", "****1/4"], ["Beverly Brothers vs. High Energy", "Blake pins Koko (hotshot), 12:54, after the Genius' scroll", "***1/2"], ["Tito Santana vs. Rick Martel", "Tito (flying forearm), 10:37. Ref catches Martel's feet", "****1/4"], ["Jake Roberts vs. The Berzerker", "Jake (DDT), 8:50. Jake nods to Bearer and the urn", "**1/4"], ["IC Title: Mr. Perfect (c) vs. Dino Bravo", "Perfect (Perfect-Plex), 12:09. Heenan trips Bravo. Retains", "**"], ["Virgil & Roddy Piper vs. Money Inc.", "Virgil rolls up IRS, 13:29. DiBiase jumps Virgil after", "**3/4"], ["The Rockers vs. Demolition", "Michaels superkicks Smash, 15:14. Michaels celebrates alone", "***1/4"], ["Death Match, Tag Titles: Nasty Boys (c) vs. Legion of Doom", "LOD 7 falls to 4, 55:59. Hawk takes all seven, two by DQ for Jimmy Hart. NEW WWF TAG TEAM CHAMPIONS", "**1/2"], ["Main: Hulk Hogan & Jim Duggan vs. Sgt. Slaughter & Sid Justice", "Hogan pins Slaughter (Big Leg Drop), 15:23. Sid never tags in; walks out with Wippleman", "*1/4"]], "n": ["15,800 at $18 = $284,400, plus $63,200 merchandise ($4 a head).", "Title change: the Legion of Doom are the new WWF Tag Team Champions.", "Changes from plan: the tag title match became a Death Match and LOD won the belts (booked: LOD by DQ, Nastys retain). Snuka beat the Mountie (booked: Mountie).", "No injuries."], "reads": [["The Legion of Doom Are Your New Tag Team Champions", "wwf-lod-champions-0720"], ["Observer report", "wwf-observer-spectrum-0720"], ["From the Road: Road Warrior Hawk", "wwf-diary-hawk-0720"]]},{"d": "1991-07-20", "p": "wwf", "link": "WWF_SUPE", "city": "Vancouver, BC (taped 7/7)", "venue": "BC Place", "type": "Superstars of Wrestling, Episode 2 (syndication)", "ran": 1, "rate": "***", "m": [["The Rockers vs. Elisha McGuire & Lindsay Snyder", "Jannetty pins Snyder (flying cross body), 6:58. Michaels out of the ring first", ""], ["Sid Justice vs. Edward James", "Sid (powerbomb), 2:07. Wippleman calls for Hogan-Sid at SummerSlam", ""], ["Beverly Brothers vs. Jerry Simmons & Lindsay Snyder", "Blake pins Snyder (reverse neckbreaker), 4:37. High Energy make the save", ""], ["Mr. Perfect vs. Shane Douglas", "Perfect (Perfect-Plex), 6:11. Heenan: IC Title vs. anybody at SummerSlam", "**3/4"], ["Main: Tito Santana vs. Warlord", "Tito (flying forearm), 12:04. Harvey Wippleman interferes", "***"]], "n": ["Segments: WrestleFest open; Hogan on Sid; WrestleFest Report; Jake's \"trust me\" vignette; Jimmy Hart with the Nastys and Typhoon; DiBiase & IRS on Virgil and Piper; SummerSlam package; Spectrum preview; Undertaker & Bearer close.", "Harvey Wippleman now manages the Warlord as well as Sid.", "Taping crowd and gate counted on 7/7."], "reads": [["Tito Topples the Warlord as Heenan Opens the Door", "wwf-superstars-ep2-tito-0720"], ["Observer report", "wwf-observer-superstars-ep2"], ["From the Road: Marty Jannetty", "wwf-diary-jannetty-ss2"]]},{"d": "1991-07-21", "p": "wwf", "link": "WWF_TORO", "city": "Toronto, ON", "venue": "Maple Leaf Gardens", "type": "Broadcast show", "ran": 1, "big": 1, "rate": "**", "gate": "14,200 · $255,600", "m": [["Bushwhackers vs. Orient Express", "Luke pins Tanaka (battering ram), 8:29. Butch's return", "**1/2"], ["The Mountie (w/ Jimmy Hart) vs. Shane Douglas", "Mountie by submission (Carotid Control), 11:03. Hart waves off the shock stick", "****1/4"], ["Dino Bravo vs. Skinner", "Bravo (side suplex), 7:06", "**1/4"], ["Shawn Michaels vs. Owen Hart", "Michaels (suplex), 12:04", "***3/4"], ["Jake Roberts vs. Haku", "Jake (DDT), 8:28. Heenan stays backstage with Perfect", "*3/4"], ["The Undertaker vs. Koko B. Ware", "Undertaker (Tombstone), 6:03", "***1/2"], ["Jim Duggan vs. Sgt. Slaughter (w/ Adnan)", "Duggan by DQ, 12:41. Adnan interferes", "*3/4"], ["IC Title: Mr. Perfect (c) vs. SATO", "Perfect (Perfect-Plex), 14:03. Heenan grabs SATO's foot. Retains", "****1/4"], ["Tag Titles: Legion of Doom (c) vs. Power & Glory", "Hawk pins Hercules (flying clothesline), 11:40. LOD retain. Animal hurts his elbow", "**3/4"], ["Main: WWF Title: Hulk Hogan (c) vs. Warlord (w/ Wippleman & Sid)", "Hogan (Big Leg Drop), 13:44. Wippleman interferes; Sid watches. Retains", "-*1/2"]], "n": ["14,200 at $18 = $255,600, plus $56,800 merchandise ($4 a head).", "Injury: Road Warrior Animal, elbow, out about one card.", "Backstage: Heenan picks Perfect over Haku; the Nasty Boys call the title change a loan; LOD say Tunney can name the place. A Hogan interview was taped and held for All American Wrestling 7/28."], "reads": [["Perfect Keeps the Gold, but SATO Steals the Show", "wwf-toronto-perfect-sato-0721"], ["Observer report", "wwf-observer-toronto-0721"], ["From the Road: Shane Douglas", "wwf-diary-douglas-0721"]]},{"d":"1991-07-25","p":"wwf","city":"Amarillo, TX","venue":"Civic Center","type":"House show"},{"d":"1991-07-26","p":"wwf","city":"San Francisco, CA","venue":"Cow Palace","type":"High-profile show"},{"d":"1991-07-27","p":"wwf","city":"Rosemont, IL","venue":"Rosemont Horizon","type":"High-profile show"},{"d":"1991-07-27","p":"wwf","city":"Syndication","venue":"","type":"Superstars, Ep 3 (10 AM)"},{"d":"1991-07-28","p":"wwf","city":"Hartford, CT","venue":"Civic Center","type":"House show"},{"d":"1991-07-29","p":"wwf","city":"Worcester, MA","venue":"Centrum","type":"House show"},{"d":"1991-07-30","p":"wwf","city":"Portland, ME","venue":"Cumberland County CC","type":"House show"},{"d":"1991-07-31","p":"wwf","city":"Scranton, PA","venue":"CYC","type":"House show"},{"d":"1991-08-03","p":"wwf","city":"Syndication","venue":"","type":"Superstars, Ep 4 (10 AM): SummerSlam main event announced"},{"d":"1991-08-25","p":"wwf","city":"New York, NY","venue":"Madison Square Garden","type":"SummerSlam '91 (PPV)","big":1,"plan":1,"m":[["WWF Title: Hulk Hogan vs. Sid Justice (w/ Wippleman)","",""],["IC Title: Mr. Perfect (w/ Heenan) vs. Tito Santana","",""],["Tag Titles: Nasty Boys vs. Legion of Doom","",""],["Million Dollar Belt: Ted DiBiase vs. Virgil (w/ Roddy Piper)","",""],["Hair vs. Beard: Brutus Beefcake (w/ Ricky Steamboat) vs. Skinner","",""],["Jailhouse Match: Big Boss Man vs. The Mountie","",""],["The wedding: Randy Savage & Miss Elizabeth","",""]],"n":["Direction, not final.","Same night as WCW at the Omni."]},{"d":"1991-07-22","p":"wwf","city":"Richfield, OH (taped 7/18)","venue":"Richfield Coliseum","type":"Prime Time Wrestling (USA Network)","ran":1,"link":"WWF_PTW_910722","m":[["Tito Santana vs. Dino Bravo","Tito (flying forearm), 10:45. Jimmy Hart fires Bravo; Bravo press slams Hart to the floor","**"],["The Rockers vs. Demolition","Double countout, 16:06. Shawn Michaels walks out on Marty Jannetty","***1/4"],["Jake Roberts vs. Hercules","Jake (DDT), 8:04","**3/4"],["The Legion of Doom vs. Haku & the Barbarian","Hawk pins Haku (Doomsday Device), 12:03. The Barbarian slow to break it up","*1/2"]],"n":["Taping crowd and gate counted on 7/18.","Also aired: the Warlord's win over Greg Valentine from the Garden 7/1 (***1/4), counted on that card."]}]);

Object.assign(ARTICLES, {"wwf-wrestlefest-preview":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · WrestleFest '91 · Busch Stadium, July 14","title":"Hogan vs. Slaughter","dek":"Twelve matches, one baseball stadium and a main event everybody thinks they already know the finish to. The interesting part of WrestleFest is everything around it.","body":[["p","WrestleFest '91 is Sunday night at Busch Stadium in St. Louis, live on pay-per-view, and the main event is the one the WWF has been selling since the spring: Hulk Hogan defending the WWF Title against Sgt. Slaughter, with General Adnan in Slaughter's corner. Hogan picked Hacksaw Jim Duggan as special enforcer on the Superstars taping that airs Saturday morning, which is the WWF's way of saying the Adnan interference everyone expects isn't going to work."],["p","Nobody in the business thinks Slaughter is winning. The Iraqi sympathizer gimmick drew its money earlier in the year, and the house-show tags with Warlord in Canada (Hogan & Duggan over Slaughter & Warlord in Calgary, 3/4* in 21:52) haven't exactly reignited it. The question is what happens after the bell, and the answer is probably standing at ringside in Saskatoon staring at the belt. More on Sid Justice elsewhere."],["h","The undercard"],["p","The rest of the card is deeper than people realize. The Nasty Boys defend the tag titles against the Rockers, a rematch of the MSG DQ. Warrior and the Undertaker go again after three straight Warrior wins. Mr. Perfect defends the Intercontinental Title against Greg Valentine, who replaced Davey Boy Smith and is coming off a neck injury, so that match is expected to be kept around 12 minutes. The Mountie faces Tito Santana with Big Boss Man at ringside to keep Jimmy Hart honest. Virgil gets Ted DiBiase. Jake Roberts gets Typhoon, who replaced the injured Earthquake. LOD face Power & Glory. Tenryu and Kitao from SWS face Demolition. Warlord faces Snuka. Haku and the Barbarian get the Orient Express. The Beverly Brothers open against High Energy."],["p","There's also a Steamboat injury update and a Barbershop segment with Randy Savage and Miss Elizabeth, which is the clearest sign yet that Savage is being kept around for something bigger later in the summer."],["h","The competition"],["p","It's the same night as WCW's Great American Bash in Baltimore. The WWF will win on attendance by tens of thousands. Whether it wins on the Monday-morning conversation depends on what Sid does."]],"facts":[["Event","WrestleFest '91 (PPV)"],["Venue","Busch Stadium, St. Louis"],["Main event","Hogan vs. Slaughter, WWF Title"],["Enforcer","Jim Duggan"],["Matches","12, plus two segments"],["Same night","WCW Great American Bash"]],"factTitle":"The Card"},"wwf-sid-is-watching":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · The Next Main Event","title":"Sid Is Watching","dek":"Sid Justice saved Hulk Hogan on TV, didn't get picked as his enforcer, and has spent the week following him around Canada. You can see where this is going.","body":[["p","If you want to know what the WWF's next main event is, watch where Sid Justice is standing."],["p","At the Superstars taping in Vancouver on July 7, which airs Saturday, Sid made the save when Sgt. Slaughter and General Adnan jumped Hogan after a TV squash. Hogan raised his hand. Sid pulled his arm away and stared at the belt on Hogan's shoulder. Then, in the Mean Gene interview, Hogan thanked Sid and named Jim Duggan as his WrestleFest enforcer instead. Backstage, Harvey Wippleman asked the obvious question (\"After what Sid did for you?\") and Sid said Hogan owes him, and he always collects."],["h","On the road"],["p","The house shows have kept it going. In Calgary on July 8, Wippleman jawed at Hogan from the floor after Hogan pinned Warlord in the main-event tag. In Edmonton, Sid squashed Brutus Beefcake with the powerbomb in a match that rated minus ***1/4, which says more about Beefcake than Sid. In Saskatoon on July 10, Sid powerbombed Hercules (**), then sat at ringside for the whole Hogan vs. Warlord main event and stared at the belt when it was over."],["p","That main event rated DUD at 12 minutes. Warlord isn't a singles main-eventer and the office has now seen that twice. Sid, standing at ringside doing nothing, was the most over thing in the match."],["h","The read"],["p","Hogan vs. Slaughter is the past. Hogan vs. Sid is the future, and it's being built patiently, which is unusual for the WWF in 1991. Sid is 6-foot-9, he's got the look, and the powerbomb gets a reaction everywhere. The question is whether he can carry his end of a 15-minute main event against Hogan, because nothing he's done so far has gone longer than about seven. The smart money says something happens in St. Louis after the bell. SummerSlam is August 25 at Madison Square Garden."]],"facts":[["7/7 Vancouver","Saves Hogan, stares at belt"],["7/7 taping","Hogan picks Duggan instead"],["7/8 Calgary","Wippleman jaws at Hogan"],["7/10 Saskatoon","At ringside for Hogan vs. Warlord"],["Manager","Harvey Wippleman"]],"factTitle":"Sid's Week"},"wwf-edmonton-injuries":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · Northlands Coliseum, Edmonton · July 9","title":"The Edmonton Injuries","dek":"The WWF drew 10,050 in Edmonton and lost three wrestlers in one night, one of them a WrestleFest headliner.","body":[["p","Edmonton on July 9 drew 10,050 for $201,000, the third-best house of the month, and it may be remembered as the night the WrestleFest card had to be rebuilt."],["p","Earthquake hurt his neck in the main event, a tag with the Undertaker against Warrior and Jake Roberts that ended when Warrior pinned him with the splash. He's out about five dates, which takes him through WrestleFest. Greg Valentine hurt his neck in the Virgil & Valentine vs. Money Inc. tag (**3/4, Virgil pinned IRS with a roll-up), and he's out about three dates. Bushwhacker Butch hurt his elbow in a 17-minute Bushwhackers win over the Orient Express that was supposed to go eight and rated **3/4 anyway."],["h","The fallout"],["p","Earthquake was booked against Jake Roberts at WrestleFest. Typhoon, his Natural Disasters partner, replaces him, and the TV is being patched with inserts recorded after the injury to explain it. Valentine was already a replacement, for Davey Boy Smith against Mr. Perfect for the Intercontinental Title, and he's staying in the match. He's sitting out the house shows until Sunday, and the match is being cut to around 12 minutes."],["h","The rest of the show"],["p","The card itself rated *1/2. The co-best matches were Tenryu and Kitao over Power & Glory (Tenryu powerbombed Roma) and the Rockers over the Beverly Brothers, both at ***1/4. Perfect beat Owen Hart in a non-title match with Heenan's help (**3/4). The worst was Sid vs. Beefcake at minus ***1/4. That's the second time Beefcake has gotten that exact rating this month."],["p","The real story, though, was after the main event. Undertaker and Paul Bearer jumped the Warrior, and Jake Roberts waited before he went in to help. More on that elsewhere."]],"facts":[["Crowd","10,050 · $201,000"],["Card","*1/2"],["Earthquake","Neck, ~5 cards, misses WrestleFest"],["Valentine","Neck, ~3 cards, back for WrestleFest"],["Butch","Elbow, ~1 card"],["Replacement","Typhoon vs. Jake at WrestleFest"]],"factTitle":"Injury Report"},"wwf-jake-hesitates":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · Jake Roberts","title":"Jake Hesitates","dek":"Jake Roberts didn't show up at the Garden, beat Earthquake twice, and then, in Edmonton, stood on the apron a little too long while the Warrior was getting beaten up. It wasn't an accident.","body":[["p","Jake Roberts' July started with a no-show. He was booked against Earthquake at Madison Square Garden on July 1 and didn't appear. Jim Duggan took the match and beat Earthquake (1/4*), and nobody has explained why Jake wasn't there."],["p","He came back and beat Earthquake with the DDT in Des Moines on July 5 (*1/4) and again in Anaheim on July 6 (*), where Damien came out of the bag and scattered the Hart camp. The feud wasn't drawing great ratings, but it was getting a reaction."],["h","Edmonton"],["p","Then came the July 9 main event in Edmonton. Jake teamed with the Ultimate Warrior against the Undertaker and Earthquake. Warrior pinned Earthquake with the splash. After the match, Undertaker and Paul Bearer jumped Warrior, and Jake hesitated. He stood there for a beat before going in. In a house show without cameras, very few people would notice that unless they were meant to."],["p","That's a seed. Jake has been the darkest babyface in the company for years, and Warrior and the Undertaker are in a feud built around darkness and caskets. A Jake turn on the Warrior is the kind of story that could carry the fall. If it's coming, it's being planted quietly, which is the right way to do it."],["h","WrestleFest"],["p","In St. Louis, Jake gets Typhoon, who replaces his injured partner. Jake promised on TV that it doesn't matter which one of them shows up. If he's involved in anything after the Warrior vs. Undertaker match, pay attention to how quickly he moves."]],"facts":[["7/1 MSG","No-show vs. Earthquake"],["7/5 Des Moines","d. Earthquake (DDT)"],["7/6 Anaheim","d. Earthquake (DDT), Damien"],["7/9 Edmonton","w/ Warrior; hesitated on the save"],["WrestleFest","vs. Typhoon"]],"factTitle":"Jake's July"},"wwf-nastys-survive":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · Saskatchewan Place, Saskatoon · July 10","title":"Nastys Survive","dek":"The Nasty Boys kept the tag titles on a count-out against the Legion of Doom, and the man in LOD's corner wasn't supposed to be there.","body":[["p","Saskatoon on July 10 drew 6,500 at a $17 average for $110,500, the softest Canadian house of the swing. The semi-main was the Nasty Boys defending the WWF Tag Team Titles against the Legion of Doom. Jerry Sags beat Hawk by count-out at 16:39, so the Nastys kept the belts, and then LOD hit the Doomsday Device on Brian Knobbs after the bell. It rated *1/2."],["p","That's the second straight tag-title defense the Nastys have survived without winning cleanly. At the Garden on July 1 they kept the belts when Sags beat Shawn Michaels by DQ with Jimmy Hart involved (**1/4). They're champions who keep finding the exit."],["h","About Ellering"],["p","The detail people noticed was Paul Ellering, LOD's longtime manager, at ringside and getting involved against Sags. The problem is that Ellering isn't on the WWF's list of managers, and he's currently in Memphis managing Andre the Giant in the USWA. Either the WWF is planning something with him that nobody has announced, or someone forgot who works where. Either way, expect it to be either explained or quietly dropped."],["h","Where the belts go"],["p","The Nastys defend against the Rockers again at WrestleFest. LOD are booked against Power & Glory on the same show, which reads like a contenders' match. If the titles change hands in St. Louis, LOD look like the next challengers. Money Inc. are lurking too."],["h","Also"],["p","Big Bubba Rogers is no longer with the WWF. He was originally on the Saskatoon card and was pulled."]],"facts":[["Match","Nasty Boys (c) vs. LOD"],["Result","Sags beats Hawk by count-out"],["Post-match","Doomsday Device on Knobbs"],["Rating","*1/2"],["Crowd","6,500 · $110,500"],["Next","vs. Rockers, WrestleFest"]],"factTitle":"The Title Match"},"wwf-owen-steals-calgary":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-09","region":"WWF Report","kicker":"WWF · Calgary, Olympic Saddledome · July 8","title":"Owen Steals the Show","dek":"13,000 paid $260,000 in Calgary for the best WWF card of the month. Owen Hart had the match of the night at home, and every angle that matters for St. Louis moved forward.","body":[["p","WWF ran the Olympic Saddledome in Calgary on Monday, July 8, the night after the Superstars tapings in Vancouver, and drew 13,000 paying $260,000 at a $20 average ticket. That's the second-biggest gate of the month behind MSG. The card came in at **, the best-rated show of the July loop so far."],["h","Who wasn't there"],["p","Calgary is Davey Boy Smith's home market, and the British Bulldog wasn't on the card because he's finishing out a full All Japan tour that runs through the Budokan show on July 27. That has not gone over well with the WWF office. He also misses WrestleFest, and Greg Valentine was moved into his Intercontinental Title shot with Mr. Perfect. Nobody is talking about it publicly, and the absence wasn't acknowledged to the crowd."],["h","The good stuff"],["p","Owen Hart vs. Rick Martel was the match of the night at ***1/2. Owen won with the missile dropkick at 18:10, and the building was with him for every near fall. SATO, one of the SWS wrestlers working the loop, pinned Shane Douglas with a layback suplex at 10:21 for ***. Legion of Doom beat Money Inc. at 17:09 (***) when Animal pinned IRS with a scoop powerslam after Virgil came down and cut IRS off at ringside."],["h","The angles"],["p","Big Boss Man beat Earthquake by DQ when Jimmy Hart got involved, and then the Mountie ran in and handcuffed Boss Man to the ropes. Expect a Jailhouse Match before long. Mr. Perfect kept the IC Title over Tito Santana with Bobby Heenan's help. In the main event, Hulk Hogan and Jim Duggan beat Sgt. Slaughter and Warlord, and Harvey Wippleman stood on the floor jawing at Hogan afterward. Wippleman's other client is Sid Justice, who made the save for Hogan in Vancouver the night before and then watched Hogan pick Duggan as his WrestleFest enforcer."],["h","The bad stuff"],["p","Perfect vs. Tito went 24:16 for **1/4, way too long for what it was. The main event went 21:52 for 3/4*. Warlord matches over 20 minutes keep rating poorly. The Rockers beat the Orient Express at **1/4, and the Undertaker beat Jimmy Snuka with the Tombstone in a short 7:34. The Ultimate Warrior was not on the card."],["p","Bottom line: the strongest card of the loop, carried by Owen, SATO and LOD, with the Mountie and Boss Man, Virgil and Money Inc., and Wippleman and Hogan all moving toward St. Louis."]],"facts":[["Attendance","13,000"],["Gate","$260,000 ($20 avg)"],["Card","**"],["Match of the night","Owen Hart vs. Rick Martel, ***1/2"],["Angles","Mountie cuffs Boss Man · Virgil cuts off IRS · Heenan costs Tito · Wippleman jaws at Hogan"]],"factTitle":"Calgary"},"wwf-garden-gold":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-09","region":"WWF Report","kicker":"WWF · New York, Des Moines, Anaheim · July 1–6","title":"Garden Gold, Road Rust","dek":"The Garden drew $543,200 for a *1/2 card. Then the Warrior and Undertaker loop went to Des Moines and Anaheim, and the shows got worse.","body":[["p","WWF ran Madison Square Garden on July 1 and drew 15,520 paying $543,200 at a $35 average ticket. That one show is most of the money the company has recorded for July. The card rated *1/2, and most of the blame goes to a casket match that the official timing has going past the 50-minute mark."],["h","The Garden"],["p","Ricky Steamboat vs. Hercules was the match of the night at ****1/4, and nothing else was close. The surprise was Warlord vs. Greg Valentine at ***1/4. The Nasty Boys kept the tag titles against the Rockers by DQ after Jimmy Hart got involved. Jake Roberts no-showed his match with Earthquake, and Jim Duggan went 18:45 with Earthquake in his place, which was about ten minutes longer than that pairing can carry."],["p","Warrior beat the Undertaker in the casket match, rated *. The crowd stayed with it, and the Garden came unglued when Warrior shut the lid, but no casket match on a house show should go that long."],["h","Des Moines"],["p","Four nights later, 4,500 paid $90,000 in Des Moines for a 1/4* card. Steamboat tore his right ACL against Skinner and was pinned at 3:47. Mr. Perfect over Davey Boy Smith (***1/2) was the only thing that worked. Power & Glory beat the Bushwhackers in 36 seconds, Warlord and Virgil went 24:49 for -*1/2, and Warrior beat Taker by DQ at -1/4* when Paul Bearer used the urn."],["h","Anaheim"],["p","The loop closed in Anaheim with 5,279 paying $105,580. The Mountie made Tito Santana pass out in the Carotid Control Technique at 19:56, the best match of the week at ****. Jake beat Earthquake again and sent the Hart camp running with Damien. Crush over Brutus Beefcake by countout rated -***1/4, one of the worst numbers on any card this year. Warrior bagged the Undertaker in the main event."],["p","Three nights, three Warrior wins, and the ratings went *, -1/4* and unrated. The program is drawing. The matches aren't delivering."]],"facts":[["MSG 7/1","15,520 · $543,200 · *1/2"],["Des Moines 7/5","4,500 · $90,000 · 1/4*"],["Anaheim 7/6","5,279 · $105,580 · 1/2*"],["Best match","Steamboat vs. Hercules, ****1/4"],["Worst match","Crush vs. Beefcake, -***1/4"]],"factTitle":"The Week"},"wwf-cuffed":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · Big Boss Man vs. The Mountie","title":"Cuffed","dek":"The Mountie handcuffed Big Boss Man to the ropes in Calgary. Boss Man is going to be at ringside at WrestleFest, and the two lawmen are on a collision course.","body":[["p","The best new feud in the WWF is two cops who don't like each other."],["p","It started in Anaheim on July 6, when Jimmy Hart interfered against Big Boss Man in his 23:52 win over Dino Bravo (**). The same night, the Mountie, also with Hart, beat Tito Santana with the Carotid Control Technique in what was the best match of the WWF's first week, ****. In Calgary on July 8, Boss Man beat Earthquake by DQ after Hart got involved again, and then the Mountie came in and handcuffed Boss Man to the ropes. In Saskatoon, Boss Man beat Bravo again (1/4*) with Hart interfering again."],["h","The setup"],["p","On the Superstars taping that airs Saturday, Boss Man says Hart cost him in Anaheim and he'll be at ringside for Mountie vs. Tito at WrestleFest to keep Hart honest. The Mountie answered that Boss Man is no lawman, and he always gets his man."],["p","That's a well-built program. Tito vs. the Mountie has already proven it can go ****. Boss Man at ringside gives the match a second story. And the payoff is obvious: Boss Man vs. the Mountie, probably with some kind of jail stipulation, sometime in August."],["h","The concern"],["p","The Boss Man matches with Bravo have been long and bad, ** at 23:52 and 1/4* in Saskatoon. Keep the Boss Man's matches short until the Mountie match, and let the angle do the work."]],"facts":[["7/6 Anaheim","Mountie d. Tito, ****"],["7/8 Calgary","Mountie cuffs Boss Man to the ropes"],["Manager","Jimmy Hart (Mountie)"],["WrestleFest","Mountie vs. Tito, Boss Man at ringside"]],"factTitle":"The Feud"},"wwf-virgil-strikes-back":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · Virgil vs. Ted DiBiase","title":"Virgil Strikes Back","dek":"Ted DiBiase's former bodyguard has spent July getting closer and closer to Money Inc. At WrestleFest he gets the Million Dollar Man himself.","body":[["p","Virgil's summer has been a slow climb toward Ted DiBiase. In Anaheim on July 6, he and Shane Douglas lost to Money Inc. when DiBiase beat Douglas by DQ in 2:13, with the British Bulldog getting involved against DiBiase. In Calgary on July 8, Virgil cut IRS off at ringside during the LOD vs. Money Inc. tag, which LOD won when Animal pinned IRS. In Edmonton on July 9, Virgil pinned IRS himself with a roll-up in a tag with Greg Valentine (**3/4)."],["p","That last one matters. A pin over IRS is a pin over half of Money Inc., and it earns Virgil the singles match he's been after."],["h","WrestleFest"],["p","In St. Louis, Virgil faces DiBiase with IRS and Jimmy Hart in DiBiase's corner. On the Superstars taping, Virgil says he's done carrying DiBiase's bags. DiBiase says everybody has a price."],["p","With two men in DiBiase's corner, the likeliest result is that Virgil gets screwed and the chase goes on. That's fine. The Million Dollar Championship is the right prop for a long chase, and Virgil has been one of the more reliable workers on the house shows. His Des Moines match with Warlord (24:49, minus *1/2) was a disaster, but nobody blames him for that one."]],"facts":[["7/6 Anaheim","Lost w/ Douglas to Money Inc. (DQ)"],["7/8 Calgary","Cuts off IRS at ringside"],["7/9 Edmonton","Pins IRS (roll-up)"],["WrestleFest","vs. Ted DiBiase"],["Champion","DiBiase, Million Dollar Championship"]],"factTitle":"The Chase"},"wwf-sws-experiment":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · SWS Talent","title":"The SWS Experiment","dek":"Genichiro Tenryu, Koji Kitao and SATO are working the WWF loop as part of the SWS relationship. Two out of three shows, they've been among the best things on the card.","body":[["p","The WWF's working arrangement with Japan's SWS has three of its wrestlers on the July tour: Genichiro Tenryu, former sumo grand champion Koji Kitao, and the junior SATO."],["p","SATO opened the Calgary show on July 8 by beating Shane Douglas with a layback suplex, ***, one of the three best matches on the best card of the month. Tenryu and Kitao beat Power & Glory in Edmonton on July 9 when Tenryu powerbombed Paul Roma, ***1/4, co-match of the night. For a company that mostly does short matches with big men, those were a change of pace, and the crowds responded."],["h","Saskatoon"],["p","Then there was Saskatoon on July 10. SATO was supposed to beat Skinner in the opener, and instead Skinner won by DQ in 42 seconds. That rated *. Whatever happened, it wasn't the plan."],["h","WrestleFest"],["p","Tenryu and Kitao get Demolition (Ax and Smash) at WrestleFest. Superstars is airing a profile on them Saturday, \"From the Land of the Rising Sun,\" with SWS footage and Kitao's sumo history. It's a showcase match, and on paper it's the right one. Demolition are big enough to make Kitao look like a monster and experienced enough to work with Tenryu."],["p","The bigger question is what the relationship is for. If it's just a few weeks of novelty matches, fine. If the WWF wants Tenryu as a regular part of the show, he's good enough to be in the top half of the card."]],"facts":[["SATO 7/8","d. Shane Douglas, ***"],["Tenryu & Kitao 7/9","d. Power & Glory, ***1/4"],["SATO 7/10","Lost by DQ to Skinner, 0:42"],["WrestleFest","Tenryu & Kitao vs. Demolition"]],"factTitle":"SWS in the WWF"},"wwf-bulldog-two-masters":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-11","kicker":"WWF · Backstage","title":"The Bulldog's Two Masters","dek":"Davey Boy Smith is finishing All Japan's summer tour instead of working the WWF's Canadian swing and WrestleFest. The office isn't thrilled about it.","body":[["p","Davey Boy Smith's schedule this month is a mess, and the WWF isn't happy about it."],["p","He's committed to All Japan's Summer Action Series, teaming with the Dynamite Kid as the British Bulldogs, and he's finishing the whole tour through the Budokan finale on July 27. That means he isn't expected back for the WWF until about July 29. He missed the Calgary show on July 8, a card that had been built around him and Owen Hart in their home market, and he's off WrestleFest. Greg Valentine replaced him against Mr. Perfect for the Intercontinental Title."],["p","Making it stranger, Davey did appear for the WWF in Des Moines on July 5, where he lost an excellent IC title match to Perfect (***1/2) and got mocked by Heenan afterward, and in Anaheim on July 6, where he interfered against DiBiase. Both of those dates overlap his All Japan schedule. It's the kind of thing that happens when two promotions think they have the same guy."],["h","The friction"],["p","The word is that there is real friction between Davey and the WWF office over the Japan commitment. Losing a top babyface for most of July, during a stretch where Steamboat is already hurt and three more guys went down in Edmonton, isn't a small thing. On TV, none of it is being mentioned. Valentine simply challenged Perfect on Superstars, and the WWF is acting as though Davey was never booked."],["h","When he's back"],["p","Davey vs. Perfect was the best thing the Intercontinental Title has done all month. If the relationship is patched up by August, that's an obvious rematch. If it isn't, the WWF may decide it doesn't need to share him."]],"facts":[["Working","AJPW Summer Action Series"],["Back for WWF","About 7/29"],["Missed","Calgary 7/8, WrestleFest 7/14"],["Replaced by","Greg Valentine vs. Perfect"],["Last WWF match","Lost to Perfect 7/5, ***1/2"]],"factTitle":"The Situation"},"wwf-steamboat-acl":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-09","region":"WWF Report","kicker":"WWF · Injury Report","title":"Steamboat Down","dek":"Ricky Steamboat tore his right ACL in Des Moines on July 5. Nobody agrees on how long he'll be out, and the WWF just lost the best pure wrestler it had.","body":[["p","Ricky Steamboat tore the anterior cruciate ligament in his right knee in a 3:47 loss to Skinner at the WWF house show in Des Moines on July 5. How long he's out depends on who you ask. One estimate going around the office is about a dozen more dates. The other is closer to ten months, which would put any return around next spring. Four nights earlier he'd had the best match at Madison Square Garden, ****1/4 with Hercules."],["p","The show itself was the Ultimate Warrior vs. The Undertaker program's middle stop, between the Coffin Match at Madison Square Garden on July 1 and the Bodybag Match in Anaheim the next night. Nobody was talking about any of that by the end of the week."],["h","What they lose"],["p","Steamboat is the best working babyface in the company and maybe in the country. The WWF runs on Hogan and Warrior on top, with workers like Steamboat underneath keeping the matches good. Without him, the card gets thinner exactly where it can least afford it, especially with a heavy Canadian swing on the schedule and WrestleFest at Busch Stadium coming up."],["p","There's also the Jake Roberts situation. Roberts no-showed his MSG match with Earthquake on July 1. Losing Steamboat to injury and having Roberts' status in question in the same week isn't great for the depth of the babyface roster."],["h","Spring"],["p","The good news, if there is any, is that a long injury gives you a natural return date. A Steamboat comeback in the spring of 1992 is a ready-made story, and the WWF has always been good at bringing people back to a big pop. Somebody should start planning it now."]],"facts":[["Injury","Torn right ACL"],["When","Des Moines, July 5, vs. Skinner"],["Out","12 cards to 10 months (TBD)"],["Return","To be ruled"],["Also","Jake Roberts no-show, MSG 7/1"]],"factTitle":"Injury Report"},"wwf-observer-notes-canada":{"p":"wwf","series":"observer","news":1,"by":"Wrestling Observer Newsletter","date":"1991-07-12","region":"WWF Report","kicker":"WWF · Backstage Notes","title":"Backstage Notes: The Canadian Swing","dek":"Davey Boy Smith misses his home crowd for Japan, Jake hesitates, Snuka misses the drive, and Jannetty's deal is coming due.","factTitle":"Filed from","facts":[["Shows","Calgary 7/8 · Edmonton 7/9 · Regina 7/11"],["Best gate","Calgary, 13,000 · $260,000"]],"body":[["h","Calgary, AB"],["p","Davey Boy Smith missed Calgary, in his home territory, because he's finishing All Japan's Summer Action Series through 7/27. The WWF office isn't happy about losing him for most of July."],["h","Edmonton, AB"],["p","Jake Roberts hesitated before helping the Ultimate Warrior after the Undertaker and Paul Bearer jumped him following the main event."],["h","Regina, SK"],["p","Jimmy Snuka no-showed Regina. The story backstage is that he missed the drive from Saskatoon, either because he overslept or because of car trouble outside Davidson. He showed up in Winnipeg the next night."],["h","Regina, SK"],["p","Marty Jannetty's contract is coming due."]]},"world-who-gets-paid":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-12","region":"World Report","kicker":"World · Money","title":"Who Gets Paid","dek":"Hulk Hogan makes more in a month than most of the business makes in a year. But the biggest single paydays of July went to two men in Memphis, and the richest gates outside the WWF are in Mexico City.","body":[["p","With every major office in the world now putting talent on a formal pay scale, we finally have a picture of what wrestlers are making in the summer of 1991. It's a strange picture. Pay works one way in Stamford and Atlanta, another in Memphis and Mexico City, and a third in Tokyo, and the only thing every office agrees on is that the headliners get the biggest checks."],["h","The top of the mountain"],["p","Nobody is close to Hulk Hogan. The WWF Champion is on a $125,000-a-month salary, and that's before his cut of the WrestleFest main event at Busch Stadium on Sunday. Through the first nine days of July he's already earned about $36,000. The Ultimate Warrior is next at $90,000 a month. Below them is a flat tier at $40,000: Sid Justice, the Undertaker, Sgt. Slaughter and the newly signed Roddy Piper. Randy Savage, retired from the ring and sitting in the Superstars booth, still draws $35,000."],["p","The WWF can afford it. Six house shows to open the month brought in $1.31 million, and the full talent payroll, $1,056,500 a month including the managers and announcers, comes to roughly 23 percent of a normal month's gate. That's the lowest share of any major promotion in the world. Bobby Heenan ($15,000) and Mean Gene Okerlund ($12,000) out-earn most of the midcard."],["h","Atlanta after Flair"],["p","WCW's numbers tell you what losing Ric Flair did to the company. The top salaries are Lex Luger and Sting at $25,000 a month, a fifth of Hogan's. Barry Windham is at $16,000 and Rick Rude at $14,000 for a part-time schedule split with All Japan. The payroll is $342,500 a month against roughly $1 million in monthly gates, about 34 percent, and the office is counting on Turner money to cover the rest. Jim Ross is the highest-paid announcer at $8,000."],["h","The best payday in July was in Memphis"],["p","The USWA doesn't do salaries. Everybody is paid by the appearance, weekly, and the base rate doubles at the Mid South Coliseum and triples at a supershow. The main event also splits 12 percent of a supershow's gate. That's how Kerry Von Erich and Bret Hart walked out of Star Wars on July 1 with about $8,200 and $8,400 for one night's work. Each of them made more from that one match than Luger has made from WCW all month."],["p","Ric Flair is the top base rate in the territory at $625 an appearance, which means $1,875 on a supershow before any main-event split. If Flair headlines Reunion Arena in front of the 16,000 the office is projecting, he'll have the biggest single night in the country outside of a WWF pay-per-view. The catch is TV. Worldwide in Jonesboro drew $3,000 on July 3 and paid out $2,150 in talent. The TV shows lose money. The Coliseum and the supershows have to pay for them."],["h","Tokyo pays by the tour"],["p","In Japan, the money comes in one envelope at the end of the tour. Jumbo Tsuruta and Stan Hansen top All Japan at $30,000 for the 14-night Summer Action Series, with Misawa, Steve Williams and Terry Gordy at $22,000. Riki Choshu leads New Japan at $28,000 for Summer Crush, with Fujinami at $22,000 and Vader at $20,000."],["p","The foreigners who shuttle between promotions are paid only for the nights they work, and there are a lot of them. Hansen and Dan Spivey are also working Memphis. Rude and Abdullah are working Atlanta. Vader, Liger, Pillman and Sting are splitting time with WCW, and Bam Bam Bigelow is due back in Philadelphia. That matters most for New Japan, whose full-tour budget of $391,000 would be over 40 percent of the expected gate if everybody worked every night. They won't, and the office is counting on that."],["h","Arena Mexico is printing money"],["p","The surprise is CMLL. The July 5 Viernes Espectacular drew 14,000 and $189,000, and the 7/12 Friday did $135,000. Konnan tops the scale at $900 a night, doubled on Fridays, plus a share of 8 percent of the gate when he's in the main event. He's made $11,520 from five dates through July 12, more than anybody in WCW. Even with 23 to 26 workers on a Friday card, CMLL is paying out only about 24 percent of its gates."],["h","The small offices"],["p","In Britain, All Star Wrestling sells every holiday camp show to the resort for a flat $5,000, and the talent splits about $2,000 of it. Giant Haystacks and Big Daddy top the scale at $350 a night. Big Daddy hasn't worked a show yet. Bob Backlund, the special attraction, is at $300 and has had the best match on both cards."],["p","In Philadelphia, Bigelow is the Tri-State headliner at $600 a show, and booker Austin Idol pays himself $400. Super Summer Sizzler paid out $5,161 against a $12,600 gate. In Nashville, Power Pro champion Chris Jericho makes $60 a card. Bill Watts makes $100 to consult. Power Pro's Nashville crowds bring in about $1,000 to $2,000 a card, so the USWA is paying to grow its next generation."],["h","The bottom line"],["p","Hogan is the best-paid wrestler in the world by a mile. But if you're a top worker who isn't in the WWF, the place to be this summer is a promotion that pays on the gate: the Mid South Coliseum on a supershow night, or Arena Mexico on a Friday. A salary in Atlanta doesn't come close."]],"facts":[["Top salary","Hulk Hogan, $125,000 a month"],["Best one-night payday","Bret Hart, ~$8,400 (Star Wars)"],["Top tour fee","Jumbo Tsuruta & Stan Hansen, $30,000"],["Top per-night rate","Konnan, $900 (×2 on Fridays)"],["Lowest payroll share","WWF, ~23%"],["Power Pro champion","Chris Jericho, $60 a card"]],"factTitle":"The Pay Sheet"},"wwf-canadian-swing-wraps":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-12","kicker":"WWF · Regina 7/11 and Winnipeg 7/12","title":"The Canadian Swing Wraps","dek":"Regina is the low point of July. Winnipeg is the best card of the tour. Sgt. Slaughter heads to WrestleFest with a win over the Warrior.","factTitle":"The Two Nights","facts":[["Regina","4,200 · $63,000 · *1/4"],["Winnipeg","10,100 · $202,000 · **1/4"],["Regina MOTN","SATO d. Shane Douglas, ***"],["Winnipeg MOTN","High Energy d. Power & Glory, ***1/2"]],"body":[["h","Regina: The Low Point"],["p","The Agridome drew 4,200 at $15 for $63,000, the smallest crowd and gate of July, and the card rated *1/4. Jimmy Snuka never made it; the story backstage is that he missed the drive from Saskatoon. Crush took his spot alongside Bushwhacker Luke, and the Beverly Brothers won when Coach tripped Luke."],["p","The main event went the wrong way for the Warrior. Sgt. Slaughter won by disqualification after Gen. Adnan interfered and then Road Warrior Hawk ran in on Slaughter. That gives Slaughter a win over the Warrior going into WrestleFest and opens an unplanned LOD vs. Slaughter thread."],["p","SATO and Shane Douglas had the best match (***). Big Boss Man looked good in seven minutes with Skinner (**3/4), and the Rockers beat Money Inc. when Michaels pinned IRS. Sid Justice lost to Jim Duggan by DQ when Harvey Wippleman interfered; the match rated -**."],["h","Winnipeg: The Best Card of the Tour"],["p","Winnipeg Arena drew 10,100 at $20 for $202,000, the third-biggest gate of July, and the card rated **1/4, the best of the tour. Every planned finish went as booked, and Snuka showed up and beat the Berzerker."],["p","Hulk Hogan and Big Boss Man beat Sgt. Slaughter and the Mountie when Boss Man pinned the Mountie with the Boss Man slam (**3/4), Hogan's best rating of July. Tito Santana and Jim Duggan beat Mr. Perfect and the Barbarian after Perfect walked out on his partner. High Energy and Power & Glory opened with the match of the night (***1/2); Owen Hart has now gone **3/4 or better in all four of his shows this week."],["p","Next: Milwaukee on Saturday, where nobody on the pay-per-view wrestles, and WrestleFest '91 at Busch Stadium on Sunday."]]},"wwf-observer-regina-0711":{"p":"wwf","series":"observer","news":1,"by":"Wrestling Observer Newsletter","date":"1991-07-11","region":"Stamford Report","kicker":"WWF · Agridome, July 11","title":"Regina: Snuka No-Shows, Slaughter Walks Out with a Win","dek":"The smallest crowd and gate of July, a -** Sid match, and a main event finish that went the wrong way three days before WrestleFest.","factTitle":"Match observations","facts":[["Venue","Agridome"],["City","Regina, SK"],["Date","July 11, 1991"],["Attendance","4,200"],["Average ticket","$15.00"],["Gate","$63,000.00"],["Card","*1/4"]],"body":[["p","The Canadian swing hit bottom in Regina. The WWF drew 4,200 paying $63,000 at a $15 average ticket, the smallest crowd and lowest gate of July, below even Des Moines, and the third straight drop since Calgary's $260,000. Cutting the ticket to $17 in Saskatoon and $15 here didn't help. The card rated *1/4, the weakest of the four nights in Alberta and Saskatchewan."],["p","It started with a no-show. Jimmy Snuka was booked with Bushwhacker Luke in the opener and never made the building. The dressing room story is that he missed the drive down Highway 11 from Saskatoon: either he slept through his wake-up call, or he got a late start and turned around after car trouble outside Davidson. The office wasn't happy, and he's booked in Winnipeg and on WrestleFest. Crush filled in, and the Beverlys beat Luke and Crush at 10:12 (**1/2) when Beau pinned Luke after the Coach got involved, which is better than anyone expected from that team."],["p","SATO beat Shane Douglas with a belly-to-back superplex at 10:43 for ***, the match of the night and their second *** match in four nights. After SATO's 42-second DQ loss in Saskatoon, it was the right call. Big Boss Man beat Skinner at 7:03 for **3/4. Boss Man's long matches with Dino Bravo have gone ** and 1/4*, so he may simply be better short."],["p","The Rockers beat Money Inc. at 19:14 (**3/4) when Michaels pinned IRS with a side suplex. They get the Nasty Boys for the tag titles Sunday, and Marty Jannetty's contract is coming due. If the belts are going on the Rockers in St. Louis, the office will want Jannetty signed first."],["p","Tenryu pinned Haku with a powerbomb at 12:09 but it rated only *3/4, the first SWS match on the loop that didn't land. Dino Bravo beat Koko B. Ware with the side suplex at 8:14 for *."],["p","The second half was rough. Jim Duggan beat Sid Justice by DQ at 10:57 when Harvey Wippleman interfered, and it rated -**. Sid has now been in two of the worst matches of the month (-***1/4 with Beefcake in Edmonton), and the ** with Hercules is the only time he's cleared a star on the loop. He's about to be pushed as Hogan's next challenger and needs short matches with strong opponents until then."],["p","The main was supposed to be the Ultimate Warrior over Sgt. Slaughter by DQ, with Gen. Adnan interfering to keep Slaughter strong. Adnan did his part, then Road Warrior Hawk, who wasn't on the show, came out after Slaughter, and the DQ went the other way. Slaughter won at 14:08 (*3/4) and goes into his WWF Title shot Sunday with a win over the Warrior, more than the office planned to give him. There's now an LOD vs. Slaughter thread nobody planned, and given Slaughter's gimmick the fans will want it."],["p","Bottom line: *1/4, the low point of the swing. SATO and Douglas, Boss Man and Skinner, and the Rockers held up the undercard, but the Snuka no-show, the -** Sid match and a main event that went the wrong way made for a rough night."]],"link":"WWF_REGI"},"wwf-observer-winnipeg-0712":{"p":"wwf","series":"observer","news":1,"by":"Wrestling Observer Newsletter","date":"1991-07-12","region":"Stamford Report","kicker":"WWF · Winnipeg Arena, July 12","title":"Winnipeg Saves the Swing","dek":"Best card of the month, a $202,000 gate, Owen Hart's fourth straight good match, and Mr. Perfect walks out on the Barbarian.","factTitle":"Match observations","facts":[["Venue","Winnipeg Arena"],["City","Winnipeg, MB"],["Date","July 12, 1991"],["Attendance","10,100"],["Average ticket","$20.00"],["Gate","$202,000.00"],["Card","**1/4"]],"body":[["p","Winnipeg saved the Canadian swing. The WWF drew 10,100 paying $202,000 at a $20 average ticket, the third-biggest gate of July behind MSG and Calgary, edging Edmonton by $1,000. The ticket went back up to $20 after Regina's $63,000 and the crowd still came, which suggests the problem in Saskatchewan was the markets, not the price. The card rated **1/4, the best of the tour, and every planned finish went as booked. July now stands at $1,575,280 with Milwaukee and the pay-per-view to come."],["p","The opener was the match of the night: High Energy over Power & Glory at 16:29 (***1/2) when Owen Hart pinned Paul Roma with the Rocket Launcher. Owen has gone **3/4 or better in all four of his shows this week: ***1/2 with Martel in Calgary, **3/4 with Perfect in Edmonton, *** with Koko in Saskatoon, and now this. He's set for the WrestleFest opener against the Beverlys, and there's a decent argument he's being wasted there."],["p","Tito Santana and Jim Duggan beat Mr. Perfect and the Barbarian at 14:22 (***) when Tito pinned the Barbarian with the flying forearm after Bobby Heenan got involved against Duggan. The twist: when the Barbarian reached for the tag, Perfect hopped off the apron and walked up the aisle with Heenan, who shouted all the way to the back that Perfect had a title to defend Sunday and wasn't getting hurt carrying the Barbarian. It got a big reaction, and the Barbarian didn't look happy getting off the mat. It was Duggan's best match of the loop."],["p","Hulk Hogan and Big Boss Man beat Sgt. Slaughter and the Mountie at 16:33 (**3/4) when Boss Man pinned the Mountie with the Boss Man slam, a little payback for the handcuffs in Calgary, despite Jimmy Hart's interference. That's Hogan's best rating of July by a mile, after 3/4* in Calgary and a DUD with the Warlord. Slaughter never took a fall, and after beating the Warrior in Regina he goes into St. Louis looking stronger than he has all month. Boss Man has now gone **3/4 on back-to-back nights."],["p","IRS made Virgil pass out in the Penalty at 12:06 (**1/4) with Hart's help, his first win over Virgil and a setup for Virgil vs. DiBiase on Sunday. The Undertaker beat Hercules with the Tombstone at 8:04 (**1/2); Hercules worked twice on the card and held up both times. Jake Roberts pinned Rick Martel with the DDT at 12:18 (**). Martel has lost to Owen, Tito and Jake in five days and been reliable in all three."],["p","Jimmy Snuka showed up after the Regina no-show and beat the Berzerker with the Superfly splash at 8:01 (3/4*) despite Mr. Fuji. Typhoon squashed Shane Douglas at 6:59 (*), which was the point, since he replaces Earthquake against Jake on Sunday. The low point was Koji Kitao over Crush with a big boot at 9:04 (-1/4*). Crush has now been in two of the worst matches of the month, and Kitao singles doesn't work; he's much better in a tag with Tenryu."],["p","Bottom line: **1/4 and $202,000, the best card of the tour and a strong finish to Canada. The two worries going into the weekend are the IC title match, where Greg Valentine's neck still needs a replacement challenger, and Sid Justice, who has to look like a monster on Sunday after a month of bad ratings."]],"link":"WWF_WINN"},"wwf-diary-owen-0712":{"p":"wwf","series":"diary","by":"Owen Hart","date":"1991-07-12","place":"Winnipeg, the night before the flight to Milwaukee","kicker":"From the Road · Winnipeg, July 12","title":"Four Nights, Four Good Matches","dek":"Owen Hart steals another show, watches Perfect walk out on the Barbarian, and wonders who gets Valentine's spot on Sunday.","factTitle":"The Night","facts":[["Show","WWF house show, Winnipeg Arena"],["Match","High Energy d. Power & Glory"],["Finish","Rocket Launcher on Roma, 16:29"],["Rating","***1/2"]],"body":[["p","Four nights, four good matches. I'm writing that down because I don't know when it'll happen again."],["p","Tonight Koko and I opened against Power & Glory, and from the first lockup the building was with us. Sixteen and a half minutes, and I don't think anybody went for a hot dog. Roma and Hercules are pros. They took everything we gave them and gave it right back. When I came off the top with the Rocket Launcher and landed on Roma, the noise was the loudest I've heard on this whole swing. Koko hugged me in the corner like we'd won the belts."],["p","It's been a strange week. Calgary on Monday was home, and I had Rick Martel, and it was the best match I've had in this company. Mom and Dad were in their seats. I could hear the old man over the whole Saddledome. Edmonton I lost to Mr. Perfect when Heenan got in the way, but Perfect is the best there is at making the other guy look like he belongs, and for eighteen minutes I did. Saskatoon was the tag with the Orient Express. Tonight was this. I keep waiting for a bad one."],["p","Then Regina happened to everybody else. I wasn't on that card, but I rode down with the crew and got an earful about Jimmy not making it. Tonight he was here, early, quiet, and he went out and beat the Berzerker. Nobody said a word to him about it. That's the business."],["p","Here's the part I'll only write in this book. Greg Valentine's neck is bad, and he was supposed to get Perfect for the Intercontinental Title on Sunday. Everybody in the dressing room knows somebody has to take that spot. I'm on the card in the opener against the Beverly Brothers, and I'll work that like it's the main event if that's where I stay. But I'd be lying if I said I didn't look at Perfect tonight when he walked out on the Barbarian and think about what I'd do with him for fifteen minutes in St. Louis."],["p","Nobody's told me anything. Milwaukee tomorrow, I'm not even on that one, and then Busch Stadium. I'll find out when I find out."],["p","Lights out. Koko's already asleep, and he snores like a chainsaw."]]},"wwf-pipers-back":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-13","kicker":"WWF · Bradley Center, Milwaukee · July 13","title":"Piper's Back!","dek":"The night before WrestleFest was supposed to be quiet. Then the bagpipes started.","body":[["p","The night before WrestleFest is supposed to be the quiet night. The biggest names were resting up for St. Louis, the card was short, and the fans in Milwaukee came out to see who'd be left standing. Then Harvey Wippleman led Sid Justice to the ring for the main event, the house lights stayed low, and the bagpipes started."],["p","\"Rowdy\" Roddy Piper is back in a WWF ring. Piper charged down the aisle in his kilt with the look of a man who'd been waiting a long time for this, and he didn't wait for the bell. For sixteen minutes he and Sid fought all over the Bradley Center, mostly on the floor, until the referee counted both men out at 16:05. Neither man stopped when the count ended. It took a handful of officials to pull them apart, and Sid was still glaring up the aisle at Piper as Wippleman steered him to the back."],["p","Why Sid? Piper didn't say before he left the building, but everyone in the WWF has noticed Sid Justice staring at Hulk Hogan's title belt for a week. Hogan defends that belt tomorrow night against Sgt. Slaughter. If Sid has plans for St. Louis, he now has a Rowdy problem too."],["p","Earlier, the Brooklyn Brawler made his WWF debut and took Virgil the full 20-minute time limit. Every time Virgil got rolling, Harvey Wippleman was on the apron or grabbing at his boot, and the Brawler took advantage. Virgil faces Ted DiBiase for the Million Dollar Championship tomorrow night, and he'll have 20 hard minutes in his legs when he does."],["p","In the six-man tag, Jimmy Snuka, Shane Douglas and Brutus Beefcake beat Rick Martel, Dino Bravo and the Berzerker when Snuka came off the top rope with the Superfly Splash on the Berzerker at 14:38. That's the second night in a row Snuka has flattened the Berzerker."],["p","The Warlord made Bushwhacker Luke pass out in the Full Nelson at 6:57, a warning for his WrestleFest opponent, Jimmy Snuka. Crush picked up the biggest win of his young career, pinning Genichiro Tenryu with a backbreaker at 8:31, the night before Tenryu and Koji Kitao meet Crush's Demolition partners, Ax and Smash. And Koko B. Ware beat Skinner with the Ghostbuster at 10:46, then danced all the way to the back to get ready for the Beverly Brothers."],["p","Tomorrow it's WrestleFest at Busch Stadium. Tonight, the whole building was talking about the man in the kilt."]],"facts":[["Main event","Sid Justice vs. Roddy Piper"],["Result","Double countout, 16:05"],["Debut","Brooklyn Brawler (draw with Virgil)"],["Card","*"],["Next","WrestleFest '91, 7/14"]],"factTitle":"Milwaukee"},"wwf-observer-milwaukee-0713":{"p":"wwf","series":"observer","news":1,"by":"Wrestling Observer Newsletter","date":"1991-07-13","region":"Stamford Report","kicker":"WWF · Bradley Center, July 13","title":"Piper Returns, Sid Still Struggles","dek":"A light card the night before the pay-per-view, a surprise return, a 20-minute draw nobody needed and a Tenryu job nobody expected.","factTitle":"Match observations","facts":[["Venue","Bradley Center"],["City","Milwaukee, WI"],["Date","July 13, 1991"],["Attendance","7,000"],["Average ticket","$15.00"],["Gate","$105,000.00"],["Merchandise","$8,000.00"],["Card","*"]],"body":[["p","The night before WrestleFest, the WWF ran the Bradley Center with a light card of six matches. Nobody from the top of the pay-per-view worked: no Hogan, Slaughter, Warrior, Undertaker, Perfect or tag champions, because the office wanted the St. Louis crew healthy. Several wrestlers from the PPV undercard worked anyway, including Virgil, who went 20 minutes the night before his Million Dollar title match. The card rated *, a step down from Winnipeg's **1/4. Business was fine for what was advertised: 7,000 paid $105,000 at a $15 average, plus $8,000 in merchandise, almost exactly Anaheim's $105,580 with no Hogan or Warrior on the card. July now stands at $1,680,280 with the pay-per-view to come."],["p","The story was Roddy Piper. He signed last week, and the office kept his return quiet. He came out as the surprise opponent for Sid Justice in the main event and got the biggest reaction of the night. The match was a DUD, a 16:05 brawl that ended in a double countout. Sid's July now reads -***1/4 with Beefcake, -** with Duggan, ** with Hercules and a DUD with Piper, and he's still being positioned as Hogan's next challenger. Nobody in the office would say whether Piper vs. Sid is going anywhere or whether Piper was just there to help a card that was short on stars. Piper at 37 isn't going to carry Sid to good matches, but he can talk anyone into looking like a bigger star than his matches say he is."],["p","The Brooklyn Brawler debuted and went the 20-minute time limit with Virgil (*3/4). Harvey Wippleman spent the match interfering against Virgil, though the Brawler isn't one of his clients. Booking Virgil for 20 minutes the night before he faces DiBiase on pay-per-view raised eyebrows backstage."],["p","Koko B. Ware beat Skinner with the Ghostbuster at 10:46 for **1/4, the match of the night. That's the same result and the same rating as their match in Edmonton on 7/9. Crush pinned Genichiro Tenryu with a backbreaker at 8:31 (*1/2). It's Crush's best rating of the month after -***1/4 with Beefcake and -1/4* with Kitao, but Tenryu doing a clean job the night before he and Kitao face Demolition is an odd call, and SWS can't be happy about it."],["p","The six-man went 14:38 and rated 3/4*. Jimmy Snuka pinned the Berzerker with the Superfly Splash, his second win over the Berzerker in two nights. Beefcake was kept to short spots, the only way he's worked this month. Dino Bravo and Rick Martel took the loss. The Warlord made Bushwhacker Luke pass out in the Full Nelson at 6:57 (1/4*). Both of them work WrestleFest tomorrow, Warlord against Snuka and Luke in the opener."],["p","Bottom line: a * card that will be remembered for one thing. Piper is back, and he went straight at Sid Justice the night before WrestleFest. The office will be hoping Sid looks better in St. Louis than he has all month."]]},"wwf-diary-piper-0713":{"p":"wwf","series":"diary","by":"Roddy Piper","date":"1991-07-13","place":"Milwaukee, the night before St. Louis","kicker":"From the Road · Milwaukee, July 13","title":"Nobody Knew","dek":"Roddy Piper on three hours in a back room, the pipes, and sixteen minutes with Sid Justice.","factTitle":"The Night","facts":[["Show","WWF house show, Bradley Center"],["Match","Sid Justice vs. Roddy Piper"],["Finish","Double countout, 16:05"],["Rating","DUD"]],"body":[["p","Sixteen minutes. That's how long it went before they counted us both out, and I'd have gone sixty if they'd let me."],["p","Nobody knew. That was the deal I made with the office: no announcement, no posters, no Mean Gene. I sat in a back room at the Bradley Center for three hours with my kilt in a gym bag, listening to the crowd through the wall. Koko got them going. The six-man got them going a little. When I heard Wippleman on the house mic, introducing Sid like he was the second coming, I stood up, and my knees told me exactly how long it's been."],["p","Then the pipes. I've walked out to those pipes a thousand times, and I'd forgotten what they do to a building that isn't expecting you. Milwaukee came unglued. Sid looked at me like I'd climbed out of his closet."],["p","Here's what I learned about Sid Justice tonight. He's big, he hits hard, and he doesn't like surprises. I poked him in the eye, I bit him, I dragged him into the cheap seats, and every time he got his hands on me I felt it in my teeth. We never had a wrestling match. We had a fight, and it ended on the floor with the referee at ten and neither of us caring."],["p","Why Sid? Because I watch the tapes like everybody else. A man who saves Hulk Hogan on television and then stares at his belt for a week is a man with a plan, and I don't like his plan. Hulk and I have been on opposite sides more nights than I can count, but nobody is going to take that belt from him by sneaking up behind him. Not while I'm around."],["p","The office wants to know what I am now: wrestler, announcer, both. Tonight I was a wrestler. Ask me again when my back stops hurting."],["p","Tomorrow is WrestleFest. I'm not on the card. Sid's not in the main event either, and I'll be watching him on a monitor the same way he's been watching Hulk."]]},"wwf-wrestlefest-recap":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"region":"WWF Report","by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-14","kicker":"WWF · WrestleFest '91 · Busch Stadium, July 14","title":"Sid Sends a Message","dek":"Hogan keeps the belt, Sid powerbombs him anyway, the Warrior goes down, and the Rockers come apart.","body":[["p","Hulk Hogan is still the WWF Champion. Sgt. Slaughter is finished. And by the time the fireworks went up over Busch Stadium, three men had made sure nobody in St. Louis would remember either of those things first."],["p","With Hacksaw Jim Duggan wearing the referee's stripes, Hogan and Slaughter fought for nearly twenty minutes. General Adnan tried to get involved twice, and twice Duggan ran him off the apron. Slaughter had the camel clutch locked in and Hogan's arm sagging, but the Hulkster powered out, made his comeback, and dropped the leg at 19:38. Duggan's three-count was the loudest of the night."],["p","It didn't last. Sid Justice came over the barricade with Harvey Wippleman behind him, clubbed Duggan from behind and lifted Hogan into the powerbomb. He planted the champion in the middle of the ring, then did the same to Duggan. Sid was standing over Hogan with the belt in his hands when the Legion of Doom hit the ring. Hawk and Animal cleared out Sid and Wippleman, and Sid backed up the aisle with his eyes on the gold the whole way."],["p","Earlier, the Ultimate Warrior's war with the Undertaker took its darkest turn yet. Paul Bearer's urn caught Warrior behind the referee's back, and the Tombstone did the rest at 12:08. Warrior didn't get up. The Undertaker and Bearer rolled out the casket and had Warrior halfway in when Jake Roberts finally came down the aisle with Damien. Jake took his time getting there. Warrior was helped to the back holding his neck, and WWF officials say he will be out of action indefinitely."],["p","The tag team title match was supposed to be the night the Rockers finally took the belts. Instead, Jerry Sags came off the top with an elbow on Marty Jannetty at 16:50, and the Nasty Boys walked out still champions. Shawn Michaels didn't take it well. He was in Jannetty's face before the Nastys had reached the aisle, the two of them started shoving, and it took a few long seconds before they backed away from each other. They walked to the back several steps apart. The Nastys, meanwhile, will want to keep an eye on the Legion of Doom."],["p","Tito Santana stole the show. With Big Boss Man at ringside to keep Jimmy Hart honest, Tito and the Mountie went fifteen minutes before Tito landed the flying forearm for the pin. It was the biggest win of Tito's year, and it puts him squarely in line for another shot at the Intercontinental Title. The Mountie got his payback after the bell, jamming his shock stick into Boss Man's ribs and leaving him on the floor."],["p","Mr. Perfect is still Intercontinental Champion, but Owen Hart made him earn it. Owen hit everything, including the Rocket Launcher, and had Perfect beaten at least twice before Bobby Heenan dragged the referee out of the ring. Perfect rolled Owen up while Owen was arguing with Heenan and held on for the three at 15:14."],["p","Ted DiBiase beat Virgil with a back suplex after Jimmy Hart got involved, but Virgil had the last word. When DiBiase turned his back, Virgil grabbed the Million Dollar Belt and ran for the aisle with it. DiBiase and IRS cut him off at the top of the ramp and were putting the boots to him when \"Rowdy\" Roddy Piper came through the curtain and sent them both scrambling. Piper and DiBiase stood nose to nose for a long moment before DiBiase backed off. Virgil left Busch Stadium with the belt."],["p","Brutus \"The Barber\" Beefcake welcomed Ricky Steamboat to the Barber Shop. The Dragon, on crutches with his right knee in a brace, thanked the fans and promised to come back the same man he's always been. Skinner interrupted from the stage to mock the injury he caused in Des Moines, and when Beefcake grabbed his shears and headed for him, Steamboat pulled himself up on one leg and stopped him. \"He's mine,\" said the Dragon."],["p","Demolition beat Genichiro Tenryu and Koji Kitao when Smash broke Kitao over his knee with a backbreaker at 10:13, but the real fight was on the floor. Crush, at ringside for his partners, pulled Tenryu out of the ring, and the two of them were still brawling long after the bell. Expect to see those two settle it one on one."],["p","Jake Roberts beat Typhoon with the DDT, and Damien sent Typhoon and Jimmy Hart running. The Legion of Doom flattened Paul Roma with Hawk's flying clothesline. The Warlord made Jimmy Snuka pass out in the Full Nelson. Haku and the Barbarian beat the Orient Express by disqualification when Mr. Fuji's cane came into the ring. And in the opener, the Beverly Brothers beat Koko B. Ware and Bushwhacker Luke when Blake pinned Luke with a reverse neckbreaker after the Coach tripped Luke from the floor."]],"facts":[["Main event","Hogan d. Slaughter"],["After","Sid ambush; LOD save"],["Tag titles","Nastys retain"],["IC title","Perfect retains"],["Injury","Ultimate Warrior (neck)"],["Card","*3/4"]],"factTitle":"WrestleFest '91"},"wwf-observer-wrestlefest-0714":{"p":"wwf","series":"observer","news":1,"by":"Wrestling Observer Newsletter","date":"1991-07-14","region":"Stamford Report","kicker":"WWF · WrestleFest '91, July 14","title":"A Strong Middle, a Weak Top","dek":"Tito steals the show, the Beverlys surprise, the Warrior gets hurt, and the Rockers shove each other as Jannetty's deal runs out.","factTitle":"Match observations","facts":[["Venue","Busch Stadium"],["City","St. Louis, MO"],["Date","July 14, 1991"],["Attendance","51,357"],["Average ticket","$20.00"],["Gate","$1,027,140.00"],["Merchandise","$205,428.00"],["PPV buys","125,000"],["Card","*3/4"]],"body":[["p","WrestleFest '91 rated *3/4, which is about what you'd expect from a card built on a Hogan vs. Slaughter main event that nobody thought was in doubt. It went head to head with WCW's Great American Bash and won the business side going away: 51,357 paid $1,027,140 at a $20 average, with another $205,428 in merchandise at $4 a head. The pay-per-view did 125,000 buys at $25, $3,125,000 in all, and the WWF's half after the cable companies take theirs is $1,562,500. The gate alone is nearly double MSG's $543,200 and pushes July past $2.7 million at the box office. The four good matches were all underneath the top three, and the three biggest stories of the night happened after the bell."],["p","Tito Santana and the Mountie had the match of the night at ***3/4, their second great match of the month after the **** in Anaheim. Tito won with the flying forearm, and the word is he's being lined up for Perfect at SummerSlam, which would be a rematch of their 24-minute Calgary match. The Mountie zapped Big Boss Man with the shock stick after, so Boss Man vs. the Mountie is still on for the Garden."],["p","The opener was the surprise. The Beverly Brothers beat Koko B. Ware and Bushwhacker Luke at 8:26 for ***1/4, by a mile the best match the Beverlys have had in the company. Blake pinned Luke with a reverse neckbreaker. Mr. Perfect over Owen Hart (***) and the Nasty Boys over the Rockers (***) were the other two good ones."],["p","Perfect retained with a roll-up at 15:14 after Bobby Heenan pulled the referee out. Owen has now gone **3/4 or better in five straight matches. The tag title match was the bigger story. Jerry Sags pinned Marty Jannetty with a flying elbow at 16:50, and as soon as the Nastys left, Shawn Michaels went after Jannetty. They shoved each other in the ring before backing off. It didn't look like an accident, and the timing is interesting, because Jannetty's contract is up and he is believed to have only one date left on it. If the Rockers are finished, this is how the office has chosen to start telling the story."],["p","The worst news of the night came in the Undertaker vs. Warrior match. The Undertaker won with the Tombstone at 12:08 (-1/4*), and the Warrior hurt his neck. He's expected to be out for a long time, and the early word is that he won't be back until after SummerSlam. That pulls the second-biggest name in the company off the house-show loop for the rest of the summer. Jake Roberts made the save from the casket, and it was noticeable again how long he waited."],["p","The main event went 19:38 and rated **3/4, tied with the Winnipeg tag for Hogan's best match of July. Jim Duggan was the special referee, not the enforcer as advertised. Hogan won with the legdrop. Then Sid Justice and Harvey Wippleman ambushed Hogan and Duggan and powerbombed both of them, and the Legion of Doom made the save. That puts Hogan vs. Sid in place for SummerSlam, and LOD vs. the Nasty Boys looks like the tag title match."],["p","Virgil vs. Ted DiBiase was *1/2, with DiBiase winning by back suplex after Jimmy Hart interfered. Afterward, Virgil ran off with the Million Dollar Belt and Roddy Piper made the save when DiBiase and IRS caught him on the ramp. It's Piper's second straight night of being used to get something over, after the surprise in Milwaukee. Brutus Beefcake hosted the Barber Shop with Ricky Steamboat, and Skinner came out to brag about the injury. That sets up Steamboat vs. Skinner for whenever Steamboat comes back."],["p","Demolition beat Tenryu and Kitao at 10:13 (*3/4) when Smash pinned Kitao with a backbreaker. Crush and Tenryu brawled on the floor and kept going after the bell, so a Crush vs. Tenryu singles match is coming. LOD beat Power & Glory in 8:33 (**). Haku and the Barbarian beat the Orient Express by DQ when Fuji interfered (*1/4)."],["p","The bottom of the card was rough. Jake Roberts vs. Typhoon was -**1/4 in seven minutes. Warlord over Jimmy Snuka was -* in seven minutes, and four of his five singles matches this month have rated 1/4* or worse. The office has to stop putting him in singles on big shows."],["p","Bottom line: a *3/4 pay-per-view with a strong middle and a weak top, and a lot happened. Hogan vs. Sid is set. The Warrior is out. The Rockers are coming apart just as Jannetty's deal runs out. Piper has a direction. Tito is back in the title picture. For a show nobody expected much from, the office will be happy with where it leaves SummerSlam."]]},"wwf-diary-jannetty-0714":{"p":"wwf","series":"diary","by":"Marty Jannetty","date":"1991-07-14","place":"St. Louis, a hotel bar near the ballpark","kicker":"From the Road · St. Louis, July 14","title":"Six Feet Apart","dek":"Marty Jannetty on taking the pin, Shawn's face, and a contract nobody's called him about.","factTitle":"The Night","facts":[["Show","WrestleFest '91, Busch Stadium"],["Match","Nasty Boys d. Rockers"],["Finish","Sags pins Jannetty, 16:50"],["Rating","***"]],"body":[["p","Sixteen minutes and fifty seconds. That's how long Shawn and I lasted with the Nasty Boys tonight in front of more people than I've ever wrestled for, and I'm the one who got pinned."],["p","I keep running it back. Shawn was the legal man, he was hot, and the crowd was right there. He tagged me in for the finish we'd talked about all week, and I got caught. Sags came off the top with the elbow, and I felt it all the way down to my boots. I was looking up at the lights before I knew the referee was counting."],["p","Then Shawn was standing over me. I've known him a long time. I've seen him mad at referees, mad at the office, mad at himself. I'd never seen him look at me like that. He said something I'm not going to write in here, and I pushed him, and he pushed me back harder. For a second I thought it was going to go. Then he turned around and dropped his head, and we walked out of that stadium six feet apart. Nobody said anything in the dressing room. Nobody had to."],["p","Maybe it's the timing. My deal is just about up, and nobody's called me about the next one. I'm not going to beg. Seven years ago, if you'd told me I'd be standing in Busch Stadium in a tag title match on pay-per-view, I'd have laughed at you. So if this is the end of something, it's been a hell of a ride."],["p","But I keep seeing Shawn's face. That's the part that won't leave."],["p","The Warrior went out on his feet with his neck tonight, Tito stole the whole show, and somebody told me Piper came out of the back like it was 1985. I didn't see any of it. I was sitting in the shower with the water running."],["p","Lights out. Shawn's room is two doors down, and I haven't knocked."]]},"wwf-observer-richfield-0718":{"p":"wwf","series":"observer","news":1,"by":"Wrestling Observer Newsletter","date":"1991-07-18","region":"Stamford Report","kicker":"WWF · Richfield Coliseum, July 18","title":"Richfield Doubles Cleveland","dek":"With no Hogan and no Warrior, the WWF doubled WCW's gate across the county line. Bravo gets fired, Michaels walks out, and the Heenan family wobbles.","factTitle":"Match observations","facts":[["Venue","Richfield Coliseum"],["City","Richfield, OH"],["Date","July 18, 1991"],["Attendance","8,500"],["Average ticket","$12.00"],["Gate","$102,000.00"],["Card","**"],["Match of the night","Rockers vs. Demolition and SATO vs. Roma, ***1/4"]],"body":[["p","The WWF and WCW went head to head in northeast Ohio on Thursday night, and it wasn't close. With no Hogan and no Warrior, the WWF drew 8,500 to the Richfield Coliseum at a $12 average for $102,000. Twenty-odd miles up the road, WCW drew 5,200 at $10 for $52,000 at the Cleveland Convention Center on the night it put the World title on Nikita Koloff. The WWF did nearly double the money with a TV taping and its B-team."],["p","That said, $12 is the lowest average ticket the WWF has run all month, and 8,500 in a building that holds over 20,000 is well under half full. The office papered and discounted to make the room look right for the USA Network cameras. July is now at $2,809,420 at the gate from eleven shows, not counting the WrestleFest pay-per-view money."],["p","The card rated **, which is about the WWF house show average this month. The two good matches were SATO vs. Paul Roma and the Rockers vs. Demolition, both ***1/4. Where this show earned its keep was the angles. They shot four of them, and every one of them moves something toward SummerSlam."],["p","The big one is Dino Bravo. After losing to Tito Santana in 10:45 (**), Jimmy Hart fired him on the spot, and Bravo answered by press slamming Hart out of the ring. That's a babyface turn in everything but name, and it removes a body from Hart's stable at a time when Earthquake is out and the Mountie and Typhoon are carrying the load. Bravo has rarely been anything but a heel in this company, and his matches haven't drawn great ratings, so this is a gamble. The press slam got a real pop, though."],["p","The Rockers story took the next step. They went 16 minutes with Ax and Smash and it ended in a double countout with all four men brawling on the floor. Afterward Shawn Michaels blamed Jannetty for not winning, left him standing alone in the ring and walked to the back. Four days after the shove at WrestleFest, it's clear where this is going. The timing is the question. Jannetty has been listed as on his way out, and nobody in the office will say whether Richfield was his last date. If it was, the payoff may happen without him."],["p","Virgil beat Dong Phillips in a squash with a cobra clutch, and then IRS ran in, attacked him and took the Million Dollar Belt up the ramp, where Roddy Piper knocked him down and handed the belt back to Virgil. It's simple, it works, and it keeps the Virgil-DiBiase SummerSlam match hot with DiBiase himself off the show."],["p","The fourth angle was in the main. LOD beat Haku and the Barbarian with the Doomsday Device on Haku at 12:03, and the Barbarian was conspicuously slow to break up the pin. Heenan screamed at both of them on the way out. LOD vs. the Heenan team was a lifeless *1/2, but the Barbarian story is the point. Coming on the heels of Mr. Perfect walking out on the Barbarian in Winnipeg, the Heenan family is starting to come apart in front of the cameras."],["p","Beefcake also did the haircut after beating Jerry Simmons, which took longer than the match."],["p","SATO has now gone *** or better three times on this swing and is clearly one of the guys the agents trust in the opening half. Roma is still being used as the guy who makes everyone else look good. Jake beat Hercules at **3/4 and is treading water while the Warrior's neck heals."],["p","1. Brutus Beefcake beat Jerry Simmons in 3:06 with the sleeper, then gave him the haircut. Squash. AAW."],["p","2. Virgil beat Dong Phillips in 2:06 with a cobra clutch. IRS attacked after and took the Million Dollar Belt; Roddy Piper took it back for Virgil. Squash. AAW."],["p","3. SATO pinned Paul Roma in 7:03 with a superplex. ***1/4. AAW."],["p","4. Tito Santana pinned Dino Bravo in 10:45 with the flying forearm. Jimmy Hart fired Bravo after, and Bravo press slammed Hart out of the ring. **. Prime Time."],["p","5. Jake Roberts pinned Hercules in 8:04 with the DDT. **3/4. Prime Time."],["p","6. The Rockers and Demolition went to a double countout in 16:06. Michaels walked out on Jannetty afterward. Match of the night with SATO-Roma. ***1/4. Prime Time."],["p","7. LOD beat Haku & The Barbarian in 12:03 with the Doomsday Device on Haku. The Barbarian was slow to break the pin. *1/2. Prime Time."],["p","8. Dark match: Big Boss Man & Jim Duggan beat The Mountie & Typhoon in 8:33 when Boss Man pinned the Mountie with the Boss Man Slam. Jimmy Hart interfered against Duggan. *1/4."],["p","Overall card: **"],["p","Next up is Dayton's Hara Arena on 7/19, then the Spectrum on 7/20 and Maple Leaf Gardens on 7/21, where Bushwhacker Butch is due back. Prime Time from this taping airs 7/22."]]},"wwf-diary-michaels-0718":{"p":"wwf","series":"diary","by":"Shawn Michaels","date":"1991-07-18","place":"A motel off the Ohio Turnpike","kicker":"From the Road · Richfield, July 18","title":"Driving Myself","dek":"Shawn Michaels on a double countout, a partner who got caught again, and the walk to the back alone.","factTitle":"The Night","facts":[["Show","Richfield Coliseum, Prime Time taping"],["Match","Rockers vs. Demolition"],["Finish","Double countout, 16:06"],["Rating","***1/4"]],"body":[["p","Sixteen minutes and six seconds and nobody won. That's tonight."],["p","Four days ago I shoved my partner in front of fifty thousand people. Tonight we had to get in a car together and drive to a TV taping in the middle of a cornfield between Cleveland and Akron. Marty drove. I don't think we said ten words before the toll road."],["p","For a while in there it was like none of it happened. Ax and Smash hit like trucks, and we were moving the way we used to. Then Marty got caught. Again. I had Smash set up, I turned around for the tag, and Marty was on the floor with Ax. So I went out after them, and Smash came with me, and I heard Hebner counting and I couldn't get Marty back in the ring. The count got to ten and the four of us were still swinging in front of the first row."],["p","We had Demolition beat. On TV, in front of a full camera crew, we had them beat, and we walked away with nothing. That's twice this week."],["p","When the bell rang I looked at Marty standing there and I didn't have anything to say to him that I could say on cable television. So I left him in the ring and walked to the back by myself. He came through the curtain a few minutes later and didn't look at me, and I didn't look at him."],["p","There's talk around the dressing room about Marty's deal. I don't ask anymore. Right now I'm not sure I care."],["p","Dayton tomorrow. I'm driving myself."]]},"wwf-martel-steals-one-0719":{"p":"wwf","kicker":"WWF · House show · Hara Arena, July 19","title":"Martel Steals One","dek":"The Model beats SATO with his feet on the ropes, Bravo wins without Jimmy Hart, and Sid leaves Boss Man in a heap.","by":"By the magazine staff","date":"1991-07-19","factTitle":"The Card","facts":[["Main","Big Boss Man d. Sid Justice (DQ)"],["Gate","4,000 · $48,000"],["Best match","Martel vs. SATO, ****1/4"],["Card","*1/2"],["Next","Philadelphia Spectrum, 7/20"]],"body":[["p","The WWF rolled out of Richfield and into Dayton's Hara Arena on Friday night. No Hulk Hogan on this one, but the fans got a main event that went nearly eighteen minutes and a few surprises along the way."],["p","Jimmy \"Superfly\" Snuka opened the show by flying off the top rope onto Skinner with the Superfly Splash at 6:11. Skinner spent most of the match spitting tobacco juice in Snuka's direction, and the crowd loved seeing the gator hunter flattened."],["p","High Energy and the Orient Express went nearly eleven minutes in a fast paced tag match. Mr. Fuji tried to swing it when he cracked Koko B. Ware with his cane behind the referee's back, but Owen Hart stayed in the fight and pinned Kato at 10:58 to send Fuji's team home with a loss."],["p","The match of the night came next, and it came from an unlikely place. Rick Martel and SATO went ten minutes at a pace the Hara Arena hasn't seen in a long time, trading near falls until the crowd was on its feet. Then the Model did what the Model does. Martel rolled SATO up, put his feet on the ropes for leverage, and the referee never saw it. 1-2-3 at 10:06. SATO argued with the official all the way to the back. Martel just fixed his hair."],["p","Dino Bravo's first night without Jimmy Hart was not a pretty one. Hart brought Typhoon to the ring looking for revenge for the press slam in Richfield, and the two big men spent seven minutes leaning on each other in a match the crowd never got into. But when it mattered, Bravo knocked Hart off the ring apron, lifted all 400-plus pounds of Typhoon and dropped him with a side suplex for the pin at 7:04. The match was ugly, but the suplex woke up the building, and Bravo left to a real cheer."],["p","Virgil's night went the other way. I.R.S. came after the Million Dollar Belt again, attempted to hit Virgil with his steel briefcase. Rowdy Roddy Piper came charging down the aisle to stop him. Piper got to I.R.S., all right, right in front of the referee, who had no choice but to disqualify Virgil at 8:06. I.R.S. gets the win, but Virgil walked out with the belt over his shoulder, and Piper and I.R.S. had to be pulled apart."],["p","Shawn Michaels worked alone tonight against Paul Roma, and the Rocker didn't seem to miss his partner one bit. Michaels finished Roma with a suplex at 10:55 and celebrated in the ring by himself. Whether that should worry Marty Jannetty is anyone's guess."],["p","The Undertaker put Hacksaw Jim Duggan away with the Tombstone at 8:06. Duggan got his shots in with the two-by-four and had the Dead Man staggering, but Paul Bearer raised the urn, Duggan turned to deal with him, and the Undertaker was waiting."],["p","The main event saw the Big Boss Man and Sid Justice fight for nearly eighteen minutes. Boss Man took everything Sid had and kept coming back, and when Sid finally started to fade, Harvey Wippleman jumped up and grabbed Boss Man's leg. The referee called for the bell at 17:51 and gave the match to Boss Man by disqualification. Sid wasn't finished. He planted Boss Man with a powerbomb after the bell and stood over him, holding an imaginary title belt over his head. Hulk Hogan's name never came up, but everyone in Dayton knew who that message was for."]]},"wwf-observer-dayton-0719":{"p":"wwf","series":"observer","news":1,"by":"Wrestling Observer Newsletter","date":"1991-07-19","region":"Stamford Report","kicker":"WWF · Hara Arena, July 19","title":"WCW Outdraws the WWF in Dayton","dek":"Martel and SATO go ****1/4, Bravo's babyface debut goes -****3/4, and Jannetty re-signs after the show.","factTitle":"Match observations","facts":[["Venue","Hara Arena"],["City","Dayton, OH"],["Date","July 19, 1991"],["Attendance","4,000"],["Average ticket","$12.00"],["Gate","$48,000"],["Merchandise","$10,000"],["Card","*1/2"],["Match of the night","Martel vs. SATO, ****1/4"]],"body":[["p","The WWF drew 4,000 to the Hara Arena on Friday at a $12 average for $48,000, with another $10,000 in merchandise. That's about three-quarters of the building, which is fine for a B-show with no Hogan and no Warrior a night before the Spectrum. The number that will get noticed in Stamford is the other one. WCW was in Dayton two nights earlier with a TV taping at the Nutter Center and drew 7,000 at $9 for $63,000. WCW outdrew the WWF in the same market in the same week, which hasn't happened much lately. July is now at $2,857,420 at the gate from twelve shows, not counting the WrestleFest pay-per-view."],["p","The card rated *1/2, dragged down by two bad matches, but the best match on it was as good as anything the WWF has done on this swing. Rick Martel and SATO went 10:06 at ****1/4, and SATO has now been *** or better in four of his last five. Martel won with his feet on the ropes, which is the setup for the spray angle on Challenge. Martel had lost to Owen, Tito and Jake in the last two weeks, so this was the right night to give him one. SATO is the guy the agents keep giving ten minutes to, and he keeps delivering."],["p","Dino Bravo's first match as a babyface, one night after Jimmy Hart fired him in Richfield, was the worst match on the card by a mile. He beat Typhoon in 7:04 with the side suplex after knocking Hart off the apron, and it came out at minus four and three-quarter stars. The finish got a pop, and the suplex on a 400-pounder is the right visual, but you can't put Bravo in with Typhoon again. Bravo needs a mechanic across from him, and the office should find him one before he's on TV."],["p","The other stinker was Virgil vs. IRS at 3/4*. IRS went after Virgil with the briefcase, Roddy Piper came down to stop it and laid out IRS in front of the referee, and Virgil got disqualified at 8:06. It was booked as a Virgil DQ win, so this one went sideways in the ring. Virgil still left with the Million Dollar Belt, so the SummerSlam story is intact, but Piper keeps getting more of the reaction in these angles than Virgil does."],["p","Big Boss Man and Sid Justice went 17:51 in the main and it was *1/4. Boss Man won by DQ when Harvey Wippleman grabbed his leg, and then Sid powerbombed him and posed with an imaginary title. The angle was fine. Eighteen minutes was too long for these two, and Sid's matches have been rough all month. Hogan vs. Sid is the SummerSlam main event, and the office has to hope Hogan can carry it."],["p","Shawn Michaels beat Paul Roma in 10:55 at ***, working alone. Marty Jannetty wasn't on the card. The news backstage is that Jannetty re-signed with the company after the show on a new long-term deal with a raise that kicks in at the Spectrum. That's a surprise to people who had him on the way out, and it changes the timeline on the Rockers breakup. With Jannetty locked in, there's no reason to rush it."],["p","Owen Hart and Koko B. Ware beat the Orient Express in 10:58 at *** when Owen pinned Kato. Fuji got involved against Koko. Undertaker beat Jim Duggan in 8:06 with the Tombstone at **1/2. Jimmy Snuka beat Skinner in 6:11 with the splash at **."],["p","1. Jimmy Snuka pinned Skinner in 6:11 with the Superfly Splash. **"],["p","2. High Energy beat the Orient Express in 10:58 when Owen Hart pinned Kato. Mr. Fuji interfered against Koko B. Ware. ***"],["p","3. Rick Martel pinned SATO in 10:06 with a roll-up, feet on the ropes. Match of the night. ****1/4"],["p","4. Dino Bravo pinned Typhoon in 7:04 with a side suplex after knocking Jimmy Hart off the apron. -****3/4"],["p","5. IRS beat Virgil by DQ in 8:06 when Roddy Piper attacked IRS. Virgil kept the Million Dollar Belt. 3/4*"],["p","6. Shawn Michaels pinned Paul Roma in 10:55 with a suplex. ***"],["p","7. Undertaker pinned Jim Duggan in 8:06 with the Tombstone. **1/2"],["p","8. Big Boss Man beat Sid Justice by DQ in 17:51 when Harvey Wippleman interfered. Sid powerbombed Boss Man after. *1/4"],["p","Overall card: *1/2"],["p","Next up is the Spectrum in Philadelphia on 7/20, with Superstars Episode 2 airing that morning, then Maple Leaf Gardens on 7/21."]]},"wwf-diary-bravo-0719":{"p":"wwf","series":"diary","by":"Dino Bravo","date":"1991-07-19","place":"Hara Arena, Dayton","kicker":"From the Road · Dayton, July 19","title":"Nobody's Name but My Own","dek":"Dino Bravo's first night without Jimmy Hart: a bad match, a big suplex, and a little cheering in Ohio.","factTitle":"The Night","facts":[["Show","Hara Arena, house show"],["Match","vs. Typhoon (w/ Jimmy Hart)"],["Finish","Side suplex, 7:04"],["Next","Philadelphia, 7/20"]],"body":[["p","First night on my own. No Jimmy Hart, no megaphone, nobody telling me when to come through the curtain. I came through it anyway."],["p","They put me in with Typhoon. Jimmy's idea, I'm told. He wanted me to pay for Richfield, so he brought the biggest man he had left. I'll be honest. It wasn't pretty. Typhoon is four hundred and some pounds and he doesn't move unless he has to, and I'm not a man who dances either. For seven minutes we leaned on each other and the people in Dayton sat on their hands. I could hear them. I've been booed my whole career, but I've never heard a building that quiet."],["p","Then Jimmy got up on the apron, the way he's done a thousand times for me. Only this time he was on the other side. I knocked him off, and I heard the first real noise of the night. So I picked up Typhoon. All of him. I've been lifting things my whole life, and I've never lifted anything that heavy in front of people. Side suplex, one, two, three."],["p","They cheered. In Montreal they cheer for me. In America they never have. Tonight, in Ohio, they did, a little."],["p","In the back, a couple of the boys shook my hand. Hacksaw slapped me on the shoulder and called me \"Frenchy,\" which I'll take. Nobody from Jimmy's room looked at me. Typhoon walked past like I wasn't there."],["p","My back hurts. My hands hurt. I need better matches than that one, and I know it. But I walked out of the building with nobody's name next to mine but my own."],["p","Philadelphia tomorrow."]]},"wwf-jannetty-resigns-0719":{"p":"wwf","news":1,"by":"Newsletter staff","date":"1991-07-19","region":"Stamford Report","kicker":"WWF · Backstage","title":"Jannetty Re-signs","dek":"Marty Jannetty, whose deal was down to its last card, signed a new contract after the Dayton show.","factTitle":"The Deal","facts":[["Wrestler","Marty Jannetty"],["Signed","July 19, 1991, after Dayton"],["Length","32 cards (×10 = 320 cards)"],["Raise","8%, from Philadelphia 7/20"]],"body":[["p","Marty Jannetty re-signed with the WWF after the Dayton house show on Friday night. His old deal had run down to its last card, and with Shawn Michaels walking out on him in Richfield the night before, more than a few people in the dressing room had him on his way out."],["p","Instead he has a new 32-card deal, which the WWF counts at ten dates a card, and an 8% raise that starts at the Spectrum in Philadelphia on Saturday. The Rockers are still a team on paper. With Jannetty signed for the long haul, there's no reason for the office to rush a split."]]}});

CONTRACTS.wwf = {"basis":"Monthly salary","unit":"per month","revenue":"July: $2,809,420 at the gate from ten house shows and WrestleFest, plus $213,428 in merchandise and $1,562,500 from 125,000 WrestleFest PPV buys (its 50% share of $3,125,000). A full month of about 22 house shows plus a PPV runs roughly $4.5M at the gate.","target":"Monthly talent payroll at about 30% of the month's gate (the WWF carries the biggest TV and production costs in the world).","how":"Flat monthly salary for every contracted wrestler, paid whether or not he works every date. PPV main-event bonus: 5% of the PPV gate split by the main event. Injured wrestlers stay on salary. Managers and announcers are on the same scale. Vince McMahon owns the company and is not on the scale.","classes":[],"rows":[["A","Hulk Hogan",125000,"WWF Champion"],["A","The Ultimate Warrior",90000,""],["A","Roddy Piper",40000,"Signed 11/6 (35 days)"],["A","Sid Justice",40000,""],["A","The Undertaker",40000,""],["A","Sgt. Slaughter",40000,""],["A","Randy Savage",35000,"Retired from the ring; commentary"],["B","Mr. Perfect",30000,"Intercontinental Champion"],["B","Ted DiBiase",30000,"Million Dollar Champion"],["B","Jake Roberts",28000,""],["B","Genichiro Tenryu",25000,"SWS"],["B","Road Warrior Hawk",22000,"Legion of Doom"],["B","Road Warrior Animal",22000,"Legion of Doom"],["B","Big Boss Man",20000,""],["B","Earthquake",20000,"Injured"],["B","Jim Duggan",20000,""],["B","Davey Boy Smith",18000,"Back from AJPW ~7/29"],["B","Ricky Steamboat",18000,"Injured, torn ACL"],["B","Brian Knobbs",16000,"Tag Champion"],["B","Jerry Sags",16000,"Tag Champion"],["B","Shawn Michaels",15000,"Rockers"],["B","Marty Jannetty",15000,"Rockers · re-signed 7/19 (32 cards ×10), 8% raise from 7/20"],["B","Greg Valentine",15000,"Injured"],["C","Irwin R. Schyster",12000,""],["C","Koji Kitao",12000,"SWS"],["C","Dino Bravo",12000,""],["C","The Mountie",10000,""],["C","Brutus Beefcake",10000,""],["C","Tito Santana",10000,""],["C","Rick Martel",10000,""],["C","Typhoon",10000,""],["C","Jimmy Snuka",8000,""],["C","Haku",8000,""],["C","The Barbarian",8000,""],["C","Warlord",8000,""],["C","The Berzerker",8000,""],["C","Virgil",8000,""],["C","Owen Hart",8000,""],["C","Ax",7000,"Demolition"],["C","Smash",7000,"Demolition"],["C","Crush",7000,"Demolition"],["D","Bushwhacker Luke",6000,""],["D","Bushwhacker Butch",6000,"Injured"],["D","Hercules",6000,"Power & Glory"],["D","Paul Roma",6000,"Power & Glory"],["D","Kato",5000,"Orient Express"],["D","Tanaka",5000,"Orient Express"],["D","Beau Beverly",5000,""],["D","Blake Beverly",5000,""],["D","Koko B. Ware",4000,""],["D","Skinner",4000,""],["D","Shane Douglas",4000,""],["D","SATO",4000,"SWS"],["E","Brooklyn Brawler",2500,"Signed 11/6 (25 days)"],["M","Bobby Heenan",15000,"Manager and commentary"],["M","Jimmy Hart",10000,""],["M","Miss Elizabeth",8000,""],["M","Paul Bearer",6000,""],["M","Mr. Fuji",6000,""],["M","Harvey Wippleman",4000,""],["M","Gen. Adnan",4000,""],["M","Coach",4000,""],["O","Mean Gene Okerlund",12000,"Interviews"],["O","Gorilla Monsoon",10000,"Challenge and Prime Time booth"],["O","Sean Mooney",4000,"Event Center, WWF Update desk"],["O","Lord Alfred Hayes",4000,"Interviews and commentary"],["O","Howard Finkel",4000,"Ring announcer"]],"total":1056500,"check":"Salaries total $1,056,500 a month: about 23% of a $4.5M month, leaving room for PPV bonuses."};

