Object.assign(ARTICLES, {"wwf-superstars-ep2-tito-0720": {"p": "wwf", "kicker": "WWF · Superstars of Wrestling, Episode 2 · July 20", "title": "Tito Topples the Warlord as Heenan Opens the Door", "dek": "Tito Santana pins the Warlord in the main event right after Heenan says Mr. Perfect will defend against anybody at SummerSlam.", "by": "By the magazine staff", "date": "1991-07-20", "factTitle": "The Card", "facts": [["Main", "Tito Santana d. Warlord"], ["Also", "Mr. Perfect d. Shane Douglas"], ["Card", "***"]], "body": [["p", "One week after WrestleFest, Superstars opened with the footage everybody wanted to see again: Hulk Hogan's big leg drop on Sgt. Slaughter in St. Louis, then Sid Justice and Harvey Wippleman ambushing the champion and Hacksaw Jim Duggan before the Legion of Doom made the save."], ["p", "The Rockers opened the wrestling against Elisha McGuire and Lindsay Snyder, and for once it was Marty Jannetty who finished it, flying off the top with a cross body on Snyder for the pin at 6:58. Shawn Michaels was first out of the ring afterward. Make of that what you will."], ["p", "Hulk Hogan had a message for Sid: if he wants the WWF Title, he can go to President Jack Tunney and sign his name like a man. Sean Mooney then ran down the rest of WrestleFest, from the Undertaker's Tombstone on the Ultimate Warrior to Tito Santana's win over the Mountie."], ["p", "Sid Justice needed just 2:07 to powerbomb Edward James. Wippleman did the talking afterward and called out Tunney by name: Hogan, Sid and the belt at SummerSlam. Sid had one line for the camera. \"The belt already knows who it belongs to.\""], ["p", "Jake Roberts, sitting in the dark with Damien's bag at his feet, told the injured Warrior to rest up and trust old Jake."], ["p", "The Beverly Brothers beat Jerry Simmons and Lindsay Snyder, back for a second beating in the same hour, when Blake hit the reverse neckbreaker at 4:37. The Beverlys kept up the attack after the bell until Owen Hart and Koko B. Ware ran them off, with the Genius hiding behind the timekeeper."], ["p", "Jimmy Hart called Dino Bravo yesterday's news, promised the Natural Disasters will be back when Earthquake is healthy, and let the Nasty Boys run down the Rockers. Ted DiBiase and I.R.S. followed with a warning for Virgil and Roddy Piper: the Million Dollar Belt is coming home at SummerSlam."], ["p", "Mr. Perfect beat Shane Douglas with the Perfect-Plex at 6:11, and the young man from Pittsburgh made him work for it. Afterward Bobby Heenan laid down a challenge: at SummerSlam, Perfect will defend the Intercontinental Title against anybody."], ["p", "After a look ahead to SummerSlam and a preview of tonight's card at the Spectrum, the main event delivered. Tito Santana and the Warlord went just over twelve minutes. Harvey Wippleman, now in the Warlord's corner as well as Sid's, got involved against Tito, but El Matador survived the Full Nelson and pinned the Warlord with the flying forearm at 12:04. With Heenan's challenge still hanging in the air, the announcers couldn't help noticing who had just won."], ["p", "The Undertaker and Paul Bearer closed the show by candlelight with a message for the Warrior, and a reminder to Jake Roberts that the Undertaker noticed how late he was in St. Louis."]]}, "wwf-observer-superstars-ep2": {"p": "wwf", "series": "observer", "news": 1, "by": "Wrestling Observer Newsletter", "date": "1991-07-20", "region": "Stamford Report", "kicker": "WWF · Superstars, Episode 2 · July 20", "title": "The Best Superstars in a While", "dek": "Tito-Warlord gets twelve minutes, Wippleman adds a second client, and every promo points at SummerSlam.", "factTitle": "Match observations", "facts": [["Taped", "BC Place, Vancouver, 7/7"], ["Aired", "July 20, 1991"], ["Card", "***"], ["Match of the show", "Tito vs. Warlord, ***"]], "body": [["p", "This was the best hour of WWF television in a while, and the rating of the show, ***, is about as good as a Superstars episode gets. Two matches went long enough to matter, and the promos all pointed at SummerSlam without giving anything away that the office wants to save for the Tunney announcement in early August."], ["p", "The main event was Tito Santana over the Warlord in 12:04 at ***. That's a long time for Superstars, and it was the right call. Tito had the best match on the WrestleFest card against the Mountie and is the most dependable babyface worker on the roster. Putting him over the Warlord clean with the flying forearm, right after Bobby Heenan announced that Mr. Perfect will defend the Intercontinental Title against anybody at SummerSlam, tells you where this is going. Nobody has said Tito's name yet, and they don't need to."], ["p", "The news out of that match is Harvey Wippleman. He was in the Warlord's corner and got involved against Tito, so Wippleman now has both the Warlord and Sid Justice. It's the start of a real heel group for him. Sid needed 2:07 to powerbomb Edward James, Wippleman called out Jack Tunney by name for Hogan vs. Sid at SummerSlam, and Sid got one line. Keeping Sid's matches short and his talking to one sentence is the smartest way to present him. His house show matches this month have been rough, and the longer he goes, the more it shows."], ["p", "Perfect beat Shane Douglas in 6:11 at **3/4, which was a much better match than you usually see from a Superstars undercard. Douglas got real offense in, and people in the office have noticed he can go."], ["p", "The Rockers beat Elisha McGuire and Lindsay Snyder in 6:58 with Marty Jannetty pinning Snyder with a cross body off the top. Snyder also took the fall in the Beverly Brothers match, which tells you how thin the prelim crew was in Vancouver. The Rockers' match was taped a week before WrestleFest, but the announcers made a point of the shove in St. Louis, and Michaels was the first man out of the ring after the bell. Backstage, the feeling is that the office is in no hurry with the Rockers now that Jannetty has re-signed on a long-term deal, which started paying him more as of tonight's show at the Spectrum."], ["p", "The Beverly Brothers beat Jerry Simmons and Snyder in 4:37, and the post-match angle with Owen Hart and Koko B. Ware running them off is setting up High Energy against the Beverlys. That's on tonight's Spectrum card."], ["p", "Jimmy Hart's promo with the Nasty Boys and Typhoon covered Dino Bravo and announced that the Natural Disasters will reunite when Earthquake is healthy. Earthquake's neck injury has him out until after SummerSlam, so the team is on hold until fall at the earliest. Bravo being fired by Hart and turned babyface has been the surprise of the month, and his first match on his own in Dayton was a disaster. The office is giving him Mr. Perfect at the Spectrum tonight, which is a good sign they want him protected."], ["p", "Ted DiBiase and IRS did the Million Dollar Belt promo aimed at Virgil and Roddy Piper. DiBiase vs. Virgil for the belt looks set for SummerSlam, although there's also talk of Money Inc. going after the tag titles, and DiBiase can't do both on the same show. Jake Roberts' \"trust me\" vignette and the Undertaker and Paul Bearer's closing promo are building toward Jake turning on the Warrior, and with Warrior out for weeks with his neck, that story is going to be told mostly on interviews."], ["p", "The Spectrum preview ran just before the main event. The house tonight in Philadelphia is headlined by Hogan and Duggan against Slaughter and Sid, the first time Hogan and Sid have been in the same ring since WrestleFest."], ["p", "Results:"], ["p", "1. The Rockers beat Elisha McGuire & Lindsay Snyder in 6:58 when Jannetty pinned Snyder with a flying cross body."], ["p", "2. Sid Justice pinned Edward James in 2:07 with the powerbomb."], ["p", "3. The Beverly Brothers beat Jerry Simmons & Lindsay Snyder in 4:37 when Blake pinned Snyder with a reverse neckbreaker."], ["p", "4. Mr. Perfect pinned Shane Douglas in 6:11 with the Perfect-Plex. **3/4"], ["p", "5. Tito Santana pinned the Warlord in 12:04 with the flying forearm. Harvey Wippleman interfered against Tito. ***"], ["p", "Overall show: ***"]]}, "wwf-diary-jannetty-ss2": {"p": "wwf", "series": "diary", "by": "Marty Jannetty", "date": "1991-07-20", "place": "Hotel room, Philadelphia", "kicker": "From the Road · Philadelphia, July 20", "title": "Long As He's There", "dek": "Marty Jannetty watches himself win on Superstars, and watches his partner leave the ring first.", "factTitle": "The Night", "facts": [["Show", "Superstars Ep. 2 (taped 7/7)"], ["Match", "Rockers vs. McGuire & Snyder"], ["Finish", "Flying cross body, 6:58"], ["Next", "Demolition, the Spectrum"]], "body": [["p", "Woke up in Philly, put on Superstars in the hotel room, and watched myself win a match I'd forgotten I was in. Vancouver feels like a year ago. It was two weeks."], ["p", "It's a good match. Shawn and I were clicking that night, I came off the top with the cross body, and I got the pin. Then they showed the replay of St. Louis right over it, Shawn shoving me after the Nastys beat us, and the announcers kept talking about it like it was a car wreck they couldn't stop looking at."], ["p", "I watched the end of our match twice. Bell rings, I'm getting up, and Shawn's already through the ropes. I didn't notice it at the time. I noticed it this morning, sitting on the end of a hotel bed in my socks. I'd like to say it doesn't mean anything. Most days it doesn't."], ["p", "Last night in Dayton I signed my new deal. More money, more years, starting tonight at the Spectrum. The boys kept slapping me on the back. Shawn shook my hand and said \"good for you, man,\" and I think he meant it."], ["p", "Tito had the main event on TV this morning, twelve minutes with the Warlord, and he looked like a million bucks. Heenan just told the whole country that Perfect will take on anybody at SummerSlam, and then Tito goes out and wins. That's how you do it."], ["p", "Demolition tonight. They beat on me for sixteen minutes in Richfield, and I'll probably spend most of tonight on my back too. That's my job. I get beat up, Shawn gets the tag, the people go home happy."], ["p", "Long as he's there when I reach for him."]]}, "wwf-lod-champions-0720": {"p": "wwf", "kicker": "WWF · Broadcast house show · The Spectrum, July 20", "title": "The Legion of Doom Are Your New Tag Team Champions", "dek": "Hawk and Animal outlast the Nasty Boys seven falls to four in a 56-minute Death Match. Sid never tags in against Hogan.", "by": "By the magazine staff", "date": "1991-07-20", "factTitle": "The Card", "facts": [["Title change", "LOD d. Nasty Boys, Death Match 7-4"], ["Main", "Hogan & Duggan d. Slaughter & Sid"], ["Crowd", "15,800"]], "body": [["h", "The Legion of Doom Are Your New Tag Team Champions"], ["p", "The Legion of Doom have been after the Nasty Boys all month, and on Saturday night in Philadelphia they took the decision out of everyone's hands. Hawk and Animal demanded a Death Match for the WWF Tag Team Championship: no time limit, first team to seven falls. The Nastys and Jimmy Hart agreed, and for almost an hour the Spectrum got a war."], ["p", "The Legion went up early when Jimmy Hart couldn't keep his megaphone to himself, and the referee gave the first fall to Hawk at 12:05. After that it was Brian Knobbs who carried the Nastys on his back. He took Hawk down with a forearm at 14:49, and three times he planted Hawk with the running powerslam, the last one at 49:18, to keep the Nastys alive. Hawk kept coming. Flying clotheslines at 18:13 and 29:01, a flying shoulderblock at 38:05, then a gorilla press at 54:06 that put the Legion at five. Seconds later Hart went after Hawk again, and again the referee saw it. That was six. At 55:59 Hawk came off the ropes with one more flying shoulderblock and pinned Knobbs for the seventh."], ["p", "Seven falls to four. After 55 minutes and 59 seconds, Hawk and Animal held the belts over their heads in the middle of the Spectrum ring, and Jimmy Hart was dragging both Nasty Boys up the aisle by their shirts."], ["h", "Hogan and Duggan Win, but Sid Never Tags in"], ["p", "Hulk Hogan and Hacksaw Jim Duggan beat Sgt. Slaughter and Sid Justice when Hogan dropped the leg on Slaughter at 15:23. The story was the man who never got in. Every time Slaughter reached for a tag, Sid looked at Hogan and stayed where he was. When Slaughter finally stretched for him, Sid stepped off the apron and walked up the aisle with Harvey Wippleman. Hogan followed him to the ropes and pointed. Sid stopped halfway and stared back. Nobody touched anybody. Nobody had to."], ["h", "Snuka Splashes the Mountie"], ["p", "The Mountie had Jimmy Hart in his corner and the Carotid Control waiting, and he still couldn't put Jimmy Snuka away. Hart got involved, the crowd got louder, and at 12:44 Snuka came off the top with the Superfly Splash and pinned him. The Mountie went for the shock stick after the bell. The Big Boss Man came through the curtain before he could use it, and the Mountie ran."], ["h", "Tito Catches Martel"], ["p", "Rick Martel has been winning with his feet on the ropes all week. On Saturday he tried it on Tito Santana, and the referee saw it this time and kicked his feet off. Tito hit the flying forearm and pinned him at 10:37, on the same morning his win over the Warlord aired on Superstars."], ["h", "The Rockers Beat Demolition"], ["p", "Two nights after Richfield, the Rockers got their rematch with Demolition and won it. Marty Jannetty took the beating for most of the match, Shawn Michaels got the hot tag, and Michaels superkicked Smash for the pin at 15:14. Then he climbed the turnbuckle and celebrated alone. Jannetty was still getting up."], ["h", "Virgil and Piper Top Money Inc."], ["p", "IRS reached for his briefcase, and Roddy Piper took it away from him. Virgil rolled IRS up at 13:29, and Virgil and Piper had the win. Ted DiBiase jumped Virgil after the bell, and Piper ran him off with his own partner's briefcase. Virgil left with the Million Dollar Belt still around his waist."], ["h", "Perfect Keeps the Gold"], ["p", "Dino Bravo had Mr. Perfect in trouble until Bobby Heenan tripped him off the ropes. Perfect hit the Perfect-Plex at 12:09 and kept the Intercontinental Championship."], ["h", "Jake and the Urn"], ["p", "Jake Roberts beat the Berzerker with the DDT at 8:50. As Jake left the ring, Paul Bearer stood on the ramp holding the urn. Jake stopped, looked at him, and gave a small nod. Then he walked out the other way."], ["h", "The Genius Strikes Again"], ["p", "High Energy saved the jobbers from the Beverly Brothers on Superstars that morning, and the Beverlys made them pay for it. The Genius cracked Koko B. Ware with his scroll behind the referee's back, and Blake finished Koko with a hotshot at 12:54."], ["h", "Sato Opens It"], ["p", "SATO beat Hercules with a belly-to-back superplex at 8:17 to open the show."], ["p", "NEXT: Toronto, Sunday, July 21. Hulk Hogan against the Warlord at Maple Leaf Gardens."]]}, "wwf-observer-spectrum-0720": {"p": "wwf", "series": "observer", "news": 1, "by": "Wrestling Observer Newsletter", "date": "1991-07-20", "region": "Stamford Report", "kicker": "WWF · The Spectrum, July 20", "title": "Tag Titles Change on a House Show", "dek": "LOD win the belts in a 56-minute Death Match, the Spectrum does $284,400, and the best matches are underneath.", "factTitle": "Match observations", "facts": [["Venue", "The Spectrum"], ["City", "Philadelphia, PA"], ["Date", "July 20, 1991"], ["Attendance", "15,800"], ["Average ticket", "$18.00"], ["Gate", "$284,400"], ["Merchandise", "$63,200"], ["Card", "**1/2"], ["Matches of the night", "Snuka vs. Mountie and Tito vs. Martel, ****1/4"]], "body": [["p", "The tag team titles changed hands on a house show. The Legion of Doom beat the Nasty Boys in a Death Match at the Spectrum on Saturday, seven falls to four in 55:59, and they're the new WWF Tag Team Champions. Nobody outside the building saw it coming, and judging by the reaction, nobody inside it did either. The Spectrum drew 15,800 at an $18 average for $284,400, with another $63,200 in merchandise, $4 a head. That's 87 percent of the building and the third best gate of the month, behind WrestleFest and the Garden. July is at $3,141,820 from thirteen shows, not counting the WrestleFest pay-per-view. The card came in at **1/2. Nobody was hurt."], ["p", "The title change is the story, so start there. Putting the belts on LOD in Philadelphia instead of saving it for SummerSlam is a bold call, and it's one the office will have to justify on TV. The Spectrum broadcast goes out on PRISM locally, so the switch will be known across the Northeast before Monday whether Stamford likes it or not. The upside is obvious. LOD are the most over team in the company, the Nastys are as good a pair of heel champions as there is, and the crowd exploded for the finish. The downside is that the biggest tag match the company could have run at SummerSlam just happened on a Saturday night in Philadelphia. The Nastys will get a rematch. The question is whether anyone believes they can win it."], ["p", "As a match, it was **1/2, and fifty-six minutes is a long time to stay at **1/2. Hawk took all seven falls for the Legion and Brian Knobbs took all four for the Nastys, which tells you who did the work. Animal and Jerry Sags barely registered. Two of Hawk's falls came by DQ when Jimmy Hart got involved with the megaphone, including the sixth, thirty-six seconds after the gorilla press made it five. The last ten minutes were hot. The middle forty were a lot of punches. A one-hour Death Match on a house show is the kind of thing that sounds better than it watches, but the people who paid were happy, and that's what a house show is for."], ["p", "Hulk Hogan and Jim Duggan beat Sgt. Slaughter and Sid Justice in the main event in 15:23, *1/4, when Hogan dropped the leg on Slaughter. Sid never tagged in. He walked out with Harvey Wippleman when Slaughter reached for him, and he and Hogan stared each other down up the aisle without touching. That's the right way to protect Hogan-Sid for SummerSlam, but it makes for a bad match. The fans paid to see Hogan and Sid, and they got Hogan and Slaughter, again. Wippleman called for Hogan-Sid on Superstars that morning, and the office still hasn't made it official."], ["p", "The best matches on the show were underneath. Jimmy Snuka pinned the Mountie with the Superfly Splash in 12:44, ****1/4, which is the best match Snuka has had all summer. Jimmy Hart got involved and it didn't matter. The Mountie went for the shock stick after and the Big Boss Man ran him off, which keeps the Jailhouse Match build going without putting the two of them in the ring together yet."], ["p", "Tito Santana beat Rick Martel in 10:37, also ****1/4. Martel went for his feet on the ropes, the referee caught him, and Tito won clean with the flying forearm. Martel had won that way in Dayton the night before. Tito beat the Warlord on Superstars that morning too, and Mr. Perfect's open challenge for SummerSlam is still on the table. Tito is the obvious name, and he's being treated like it."], ["p", "SATO beat Hercules with a superplex in 8:17, ***1/2. That's five of his last six at *** or better. The Beverly Brothers beat High Energy in 12:54 at ***1/2, Blake pinning Koko with a hotshot after the Genius hit Koko with his scroll. That pays off the High Energy save that aired on Superstars the same morning."], ["p", "The Rockers beat Demolition in 15:14, ***1/4. Jannetty took the beating, Michaels got the hot tag and the pin with the superkick, and then Michaels celebrated on the turnbuckle by himself while Jannetty was still on the mat. That's three shows running where Michaels has either worked alone or celebrated alone. Jannetty re-signed on Friday. Somebody should tell him."], ["p", "Virgil and Roddy Piper beat Money Inc. in 13:29, **3/4, when Virgil rolled up IRS after Piper took the briefcase away. DiBiase jumped Virgil after, and Piper ran him off. Virgil got his win back after the DQ loss in Dayton and still has the Million Dollar Belt."], ["p", "Mr. Perfect beat Dino Bravo in 12:09 with the Perfect-Plex after Bobby Heenan tripped Bravo, **. It's not good, but after Bravo's minus four and three-quarter stars against Typhoon on Friday, it's an improvement of almost seven stars, and that's what putting him in with a worker gets you."], ["p", "Jake Roberts beat the Berzerker with the DDT in 8:50, **1/4. The match didn't matter. What happened after did. Paul Bearer was on the ramp holding the urn, and Jake stopped, looked at him, and nodded. It's the first time Jake has shown anything but hate for Bearer and the Undertaker in public, and it was the most talked-about moment on the show after the title change. With the Warrior out, the office is telling that story through Jake, and it's working."], ["p", "1. SATO pinned Hercules with a belly-to-back superplex in 8:17. ***1/2"], ["p", "2. Jimmy Snuka pinned the Mountie with the Superfly Splash in 12:44. Jimmy Hart interfered. Boss Man ran off the Mountie after. ****1/4"], ["p", "3. The Beverly Brothers beat High Energy when Blake pinned Koko B. Ware with a hotshot in 12:54, after the Genius' scroll shot. ***1/2"], ["p", "4. Tito Santana pinned Rick Martel with the flying forearm in 10:37. The referee caught Martel's feet on the ropes. ****1/4"], ["p", "5. Jake Roberts pinned the Berzerker with the DDT in 8:50. Paul Bearer on the ramp with the urn; Jake nodded to him. **1/4"], ["p", "6. IC Title: Mr. Perfect pinned Dino Bravo with the Perfect-Plex in 12:09 after Bobby Heenan tripped Bravo. Perfect retains. **"], ["p", "7. Virgil & Roddy Piper beat Money Inc. when Virgil rolled up IRS in 13:29 after Piper took the briefcase. DiBiase jumped Virgil after. **3/4"], ["p", "8. The Rockers beat Demolition when Shawn Michaels superkicked Smash in 15:14. Michaels celebrated alone. ***1/4"], ["p", "9. Death Match, WWF Tag Team Title: The Legion of Doom beat the Nasty Boys, seven falls to four, in 55:59. Hawk took all seven, two by DQ for Jimmy Hart's interference; Knobbs took all four. NEW CHAMPIONS. **1/2"], ["p", "10. Hulk Hogan & Jim Duggan beat Sgt. Slaughter & Sid Justice when Hogan pinned Slaughter with the Big Leg Drop in 15:23. Sid never tagged in and walked out with Wippleman. *1/4"], ["p", "Overall card: **1/2"], ["p", "Next is Toronto on Sunday at Maple Leaf Gardens, Hogan against the Warlord."]]}, "wwf-diary-hawk-0720": {"p": "wwf", "series": "diary", "by": "Road Warrior Hawk", "date": "1991-07-20", "place": "The car, leaving Philadelphia", "kicker": "From the Road · Philadelphia, July 20", "title": "Fifty-Six Minutes", "dek": "Road Warrior Hawk on seven falls, Jimmy Hart's megaphone, and a belt riding in the front seat.", "factTitle": "The Night", "facts": [["Show", "The Spectrum, broadcast house show"], ["Match", "Death Match vs. the Nasty Boys"], ["Finish", "Flying shoulderblock, 55:59"], ["Rating", "**1/2"], ["Next", "Toronto, 7/21"]], "body": [["p", "Fifty-six minutes. I've had shorter nights in jail."], ["p", "Animal said it in the locker room after, with a bag of ice on each shoulder: we came here to win the belts, not to win a marathon. He's right, but nobody told Knobbs that. Three times that fat boy picked me up and drove me through the mat with the powerslam, and three times I looked up at the lights and thought, that's it, they're going to drag this into next week. Every time, I got up. That's the whole trick. You get up."], ["p", "Jimmy Hart did us two favors tonight and he'll never admit it. He can't keep his hands off that megaphone. The first time, the ref saw it and gave us the fall. Late, when we were up five to four and I could barely see, he did it again, right in front of the ref, and that was six. I almost felt bad for Knobbs. Almost."], ["p", "The last one I don't really remember. I remember coming off the ropes. I remember Knobbs being on his back. I remember Animal's hand on the back of my neck, holding me up, and the belt in my other hand. The Spectrum was louder than anything I've heard since we came to this company. This town loves a fight, and we gave them one."], ["p", "The office wasn't thrilled. One of the agents said the words \"SummerSlam\" to me three times before I got my boots off. Let them worry about the television. We were told we'd get a shot at the Nasty Boys when we earned it. We've been earning it all month. Tonight we stopped asking."], ["p", "Sags didn't say a word in the back. Knobbs came over and shook my hand, which surprised me. He's a lot tougher than he looks, and he looks pretty tough. They'll get their rematch. They should. And then they'll lose that one too."], ["p", "Hogan came by while I was getting taped up. He didn't say much, just \"about time, brother,\" and walked off to go do his thing with Sid, which from what I saw was nothing. Sid never came in the ring. Big man, big mouth, and he hasn't laid a hand on Hogan since St. Louis. Somebody's going to have to make that happen."], ["p", "Animal's already asleep in the back of the car. Toronto tomorrow. I've got the belt in the front seat with me, seatbelt on and everything."]]}});
