Object.assign(ARTICLES, {"wwf-challenge-ep1-martel-0721": {"p": "wwf", "kicker": "WWF · Wrestling Challenge, Episode 1 · July 21", "title": "Martel Makes the Superfly Submit", "dek": "Rick Martel thumbs Jimmy Snuka in the eye and locks on the Boston crab, Dino Bravo talks for himself, and Piper answers the audit.", "by": "By the magazine staff", "date": "1991-07-21", "factTitle": "The Card", "facts": [["Taped", "Richfield Coliseum, 7/18"], ["Main", "Rick Martel d. Jimmy Snuka"], ["Finish", "Boston crab, 9:47"], ["Card", "**1/2"]], "body": [["p", "Gorilla Monsoon and Lord Alfred Hayes opened the first Wrestling Challenge of the summer from Richfield, and Hayes summed up the main event before the bell: the Model, who never lets a hair fall out of place, against a man who throws himself off the top rope for a living."], ["p", "Demolition got things started, with Crush standing guard on the apron. Smash broke Corey Sweet over his knee with a backbreaker and pinned him at 4:03. Three men, two belts, and Hayes wasn't the only one doing the arithmetic."], ["p", "Then came the interview people will be talking about. Dino Bravo, still sweating from his match with Tito Santana and with nobody standing behind him, told Mean Gene Okerlund that Jimmy Hart had treated him like a dog for years. \"I am not his dog, Gene.\" From now on, Bravo said, he talks for himself. The Richfield crowd cheered him, and Gorilla admitted it was the biggest reaction Bravo has had in years."], ["p", "The Big Boss Man needed 2:35 and one Boss Man Slam to put away Dong Phillips. He didn't name anybody afterward. He didn't have to."], ["p", "Power and Glory beat Willard Moody and Dick Osborne, who was back for a second helping after the Demolition match, when Paul Roma came off the top with a flying bodypress at 4:28. Hercules and Roma posed on the turnbuckles like men who'd just won the tag titles. Tonight in Toronto they get the chance to."], ["p", "Brutus Beefcake opened the Barber Shop for Virgil, with the Million Dollar Belt over his shoulder, and Rowdy Roddy Piper leaning on the pole. Virgil said he carried Ted DiBiase's bags for years, and the belt stays with him until DiBiase takes it back in the ring, not from behind. Piper took care of Irwin R. Schyster's audit: he's a Scotsman, he hasn't spent a dollar since 1974, and the only pages in his books with I.R.S.'s name on them are IOUs for a beating. His message to DiBiase: bring your checkbook to SummerSlam, you'll need it for the hospital bill."], ["p", "Sean Mooney's Maple Leaf Gardens Report brought the news from Philadelphia. The Legion of Doom beat the Nasty Boys after almost an hour on Saturday night and are the new WWF Tag Team Champions. Hawk and Animal make their first defense tonight at the Gardens against Power and Glory, on a card headlined by Hulk Hogan against the Warlord, with Sid Justice in the Warlord's corner."], ["p", "The main event was the best thing on the show. Jimmy Snuka took it to Rick Martel early with chops and a headbutt, and Martel bailed to the floor to fix his hair. Martel took over with a knee on the way back in and went to work on the back, until the Superfly fought back with chops in the corner and a diving headbutt that nearly ended it. Snuka slammed Martel and went up top, but Martel rolled clear, and with the referee checking on Snuka, Martel went to the eye. The Boston crab went on in the middle of the ring, and after two reaches for the ropes, Snuka submitted at 9:47."], ["p", "Martel checked his face in a hand mirror before he would talk to Gene. Not a mark on it. Class beats a crowd, he said, and then he had a word for SATO: excitement fades, but arrogance is forever. He sprayed the cologne into the air and walked off through the mist."]]}, "wwf-observer-challenge-ep1": {"p": "wwf", "series": "observer", "news": 1, "by": "Wrestling Observer Newsletter", "date": "1991-07-21", "region": "Stamford Report", "kicker": "WWF · Wrestling Challenge, Episode 1 · July 21", "title": "Three Promos and a Good Main", "dek": "Martel-Snuka is the match, but Bravo's first words on his own and Piper's Barber Shop are what the hour is remembered for.", "factTitle": "Match observations", "facts": [["Taped", "Richfield Coliseum, 7/18"], ["Aired", "July 21, 1991"], ["Card", "**1/2"], ["Match of the show", "Martel vs. Snuka, **1/2"]], "body": [["p", "A solid, unspectacular hour at **1/2, which is about what you expect from the first show off a four-episode taping. One real match, three squashes, and three talking segments that did more for the company than the wrestling did."], ["p", "Rick Martel beat Jimmy Snuka in 9:47 at **1/2 with the Boston crab after a thumb to the eye while the referee was checking Snuka. It was a good TV match, and it was laid out to protect Snuka, who never got pinned and was making his comeback when Martel cheated. Martel is having his best run in a long time. The night after this was taped, he beat SATO with his feet on the ropes in Dayton in the best match on that card, and his promo here went right at SATO again. The office clearly sees Martel vs. SATO as a program. Snuka, for his part, went into the Spectrum the night before this aired and beat the Mountie at ****1/4, so the loss here costs him nothing."], ["p", "The segment people will remember is Dino Bravo with Gene Okerlund. It was taped maybe twenty minutes after Jimmy Hart fired him on the Prime Time match with Tito Santana, and the Richfield crowd cheered him, which almost never happens. Bravo isn't a natural talker and the promo was short and simple, which was the right way to do it. The babyface turn has been the surprise of the month. His first match on his own in Dayton was a disaster and he lost to Mr. Perfect at the Spectrum, but the reaction here says there's something there. He's in front of a Canadian crowd in Toronto tonight against Skinner, which is the easiest win they could give him."], ["p", "The Barber Shop with Virgil and Roddy Piper was the best talking on the show. Piper is still the best promo in the company when he wants to be, and the Scotsman-who-never-spends-a-dollar bit answering I.R.S.'s audit line was funny. Virgil was better than expected. The Million Dollar Belt story is headed to SummerSlam, and Virgil and Piper beat Money Inc. at the Spectrum on Saturday, so this lined up nicely, even though it was taped two days before that match."], ["p", "Sean Mooney's Maple Leaf Gardens Report was the only part of the show that knew the Legion of Doom are champions. It was shot after the Spectrum, and it sells LOD's first defense tonight against Power & Glory. Power & Glory beat Willard Moody and Dick Osborne earlier in the hour with Roma's flying bodypress in 4:28, so at least the challengers looked like a team going somewhere. Nobody believes the belts are changing hands tonight."], ["p", "Demolition beat Corey Sweet and Osborne in 4:03, Smash pinning Sweet with a backbreaker, with Crush on the apron. That's three men for two spots and no titles in the picture now that LOD have the belts. The Richfield double countout with the Rockers airs on Prime Time tomorrow night, and that's the program for Demolition right now. Osborne took the fall in the Power & Glory match too. Two jobs in one hour at the same taping tells you how short the enhancement crew was in Ohio."], ["p", "The Big Boss Man beat Dong Phillips in 2:35. That's a placeholder. Boss Man's next real TV is the challenge to the Mountie on Superstars next Saturday, and they kept the Mountie's name off this one on purpose."], ["h", "Results"], ["p", "1. Demolition beat Corey Sweet & Dick Osborne in 4:03 when Smash pinned Sweet with a backbreaker."], ["p", "2. Big Boss Man pinned Dong Phillips in 2:35 with the Boss Man Slam."], ["p", "3. Power & Glory beat Willard Moody & Dick Osborne in 4:28 when Roma pinned Osborne with a flying bodypress."], ["p", "4. Rick Martel beat Jimmy Snuka by submission in 9:47 with the Boston crab. **1/2 Overall show: **1/2"]]}, "wwf-diary-snuka-0721": {"p": "wwf", "series": "diary", "by": "Jimmy Snuka", "date": "1991-07-21", "place": "Hotel room, Philadelphia", "kicker": "From the Road · Philadelphia, July 21", "title": "Martel Can Keep His Mirror", "dek": "Jimmy Snuka beat the Mountie on Saturday night and watched himself lose to Rick Martel on Sunday morning.", "factTitle": "The Night", "facts": [["Show", "Wrestling Challenge Ep. 1 (taped 7/18)"], ["Match", "Jimmy Snuka vs. Rick Martel"], ["Finish", "Boston crab, 9:47"], ["Next", "San Francisco, 7/26"]], "body": [["p", "Last night I beat the Mountie in front of fifteen thousand people at the Spectrum. This morning I turned on the television in my hotel room and watched Rick Martel make me quit."], ["p", "That's this business. They tape the TV on Thursday and show it on Sunday, and by the time the people see it, you've lived three more nights. In Richfield I lost. In Dayton I beat Skinner. Last night I went off the top onto the Mountie and the building came down. This morning I lost again."], ["p", "The match was good. I watched it all the way through, which I don't always do. I had Martel going. The people in Ohio were with me, and when I went up top I thought I had him. He moved. I know he was going to move, because I've been doing this a long time and they all move now. Then the thumb in the eye. I didn't see it on the tape either, the camera was on the referee. I felt it, though."], ["p", "The crab I could have held longer. I reached twice. My back said no the second time, and you have to listen to your back when you are my age."], ["p", "Martel talks like a man who's never been hit. He talked about my age on the television. That's okay. He's not a young man either, and he smells like a department store."], ["p", "Bravo was on the show too. I've known Dino a long time. I never heard anybody cheer him before. I hope it lasts for him."], ["p", "No booking tonight. Toronto goes on without me. Next week I'm in San Francisco. My back will be ready. Martel can keep his mirror."]]}, "wwf-toronto-perfect-sato-0721": {"p": "wwf", "kicker": "WWF · Maple Leaf Gardens, Toronto · July 21", "title": "Perfect Keeps the Gold, but SATO Steals the Show", "dek": "Heenan grabs SATO's foot to save the Intercontinental Title, LOD survive their first defense, and Sid watches Hogan from the Warlord's corner.", "by": "By the magazine staff", "date": "1991-07-21", "factTitle": "The Card", "facts": [["Attendance", "14,200"], ["Average ticket", "$18.00"], ["Gate", "$255,600"], ["Merchandise", "$56,800"], ["Main", "Hulk Hogan d. Warlord"], ["Card", "**"]], "body": [["p", "The Legion of Doom were the new champions, Hulk Hogan was in the main event, and Sid Justice was standing in the Warlord's corner. But the match Toronto will be talking about this week was the Intercontinental Title match. SATO took Mr. Perfect to the limit for fourteen minutes, and had him beaten more than once. When SATO went to the top rope, Bobby Heenan reached up and grabbed his foot, and Perfect pulled him down into the Perfect-Plex for the pin at 14:03. Perfect is still the champion. SATO left to one of the biggest ovations of the night."], ["p", "The Mountie had already set the bar. Shane Douglas gave him everything he had, and for eleven minutes the Gardens believed the kid from Pittsburgh might pull it off. Then the Mountie locked in the Carotid Control, and Douglas faded and submitted at 11:03. Afterward the Mountie went for the shock stick, but Jimmy Hart grabbed his arm and told him to save it."], ["h", "First Defense for the Legion"], ["p", "Before the tag title match, Jimmy Hart and the Nasty Boys told Sean Mooney they were robbed in Philadelphia. Sags carried an empty belt bag and Knobbs called the Legion's win a loan. Hawk and Animal answered in the ring. Power and Glory kept them busy for more than eleven minutes, but Hawk came off the ropes with the flying clothesline on Hercules and pinned him at 11:40. The Legion of Doom are still the champions."], ["p", "Animal came through the curtain favoring his right elbow, but he and Hawk had an answer for the Nasty Boys anyway: Jack Tunney names the place, and they'll be there. Bring the megaphone, Jimmy."], ["h", "Hogan Beats the Warlord, Sid Watches"], ["p", "Harvey Wippleman told Mooney that Sid Justice would be in the Warlord's corner tonight to watch and learn. Sid had one line for the champion: \"Look up, Hogan. I'll be there.\""], ["p", "Sid did exactly that. He stood in the corner the whole match and never touched the ropes. Wippleman was not so patient. He got involved when the Warlord had Hogan in trouble, but Hogan Hulked up, dropped the big leg and pinned the Warlord at 13:44. When it was over, Sid stepped up on the apron. Hogan pointed at him, then at the belt. Wippleman pulled Sid down before anybody threw a punch."], ["h", "Heenan Chooses"], ["p", "Backstage, Haku came looking for Bobby Heenan before his match with Jake Roberts. Heenan was standing outside Mr. Perfect's locker room and told Haku he'd be watching on the monitor. Haku looked at Heenan, looked at the Intercontinental Title, and walked off alone. Jake Roberts beat him with the DDT at 8:28, and Heenan never came out."], ["h", "The Rest of the Card"], ["p", "Shawn Michaels told Mooney that Marty Jannetty had the night off, and that Owen Hart would find out what one Rocker could do by himself. \"Figure of speech,\" he added. Michaels and Owen then had one of the best matches of the night, and Michaels won it with a suplex at 12:04. Owen offered his hand before the bell. Michaels took his time before he shook it."], ["p", "The Undertaker beat Koko B. Ware with the Tombstone at 6:03 after a better fight than anybody expected from Koko."], ["p", "Hacksaw Jim Duggan beat Sgt. Slaughter by disqualification at 12:41 when General Adnan came in with the flag pole. Duggan cleared them both out with the 2x4."], ["p", "Dino Bravo, wrestling in Canada for the first time since Jimmy Hart fired him, told the Toronto fans \"ce soir, c'est pour vous,\" and then pinned Skinner with the side suplex at 7:06. The crowd was with him all the way."], ["p", "The Bushwhackers opened the show. Butch is back from his elbow injury, and he and Luke celebrated by licking Sean Mooney, then beat the Orient Express when Luke pinned Tanaka with the battering ram at 8:29."]]}, "wwf-observer-toronto-0721": {"p": "wwf", "series": "observer", "news": 1, "by": "Wrestling Observer Newsletter", "date": "1991-07-21", "region": "Stamford Report", "kicker": "WWF · Maple Leaf Gardens, July 21", "title": "Toronto: Two Great Matches and a Bad Main Event", "dek": "Perfect-SATO and Mountie-Douglas both hit ****1/4, Animal hurts his elbow before Chicago, and Hogan-Warlord goes -*1/2.", "factTitle": "Match observations", "facts": [["Venue", "Maple Leaf Gardens"], ["City", "Toronto, ON"], ["Attendance", "14,200"], ["Gate", "$255,600"], ["Merchandise", "$56,800 ($4 per fan)"], ["Card", "**"]], "body": [["p", "The night after the Spectrum, the WWF drew 14,200 to the Gardens, about 1,400 short of a full house and 1,600 under Philadelphia, at the same average ticket. For a Sunday night with a Hogan main event the office will take it, but it's worth noting that the Warlord on top didn't move tickets the way the Death Match did in Philly. Merchandise did $4 a head again, which is strong."], ["p", "Two matches carried the show, and neither was the main event."], ["p", "Mr. Perfect beat SATO in 14:03 at ****1/4 with the Perfect-Plex after Bobby Heenan grabbed SATO's foot on the top rope. This was the match of the night by most accounts, and it's the best SATO has looked in the company. He beat Hercules clean in Philadelphia the night before, he's had a run of great matches all month, and the Martel program is waiting for him on TV. Perfect needed Heenan again, which has been the pattern every time he's defended this month: Owen at WrestleFest, Bravo at the Spectrum, now SATO. That keeps every challenger strong going into the SummerSlam open challenge, but at some point Perfect has to win one on his own or the act wears thin."], ["p", "The Mountie beat Shane Douglas by submission with the Carotid Control in 11:03, also ****1/4. Douglas is one of the most underrated workers on the roster and has now had back-to-back strong matches with the Mountie and Perfect. The Mountie has been excellent since WrestleFest, when he and Tito had the best match on that show. Jimmy Hart stopped him from using the shock stick afterward, which is the right way to hold that back until the Big Boss Man finally gets his hands on him."], ["p", "Shawn Michaels beat Owen Hart in 12:04 at ***3/4 with a suplex, and it was a great match between two guys who should be wrestling each other for years. The backstage interview before it was the interesting part. Michaels was alone, said Marty Jannetty had the night off, and talked about what \"one Rocker\" could do by himself before calling it a figure of speech. There's no question where this team is headed. The office still wants to take its time, and Jannetty only just re-signed, but every week Michaels looks more like a single."], ["p", "The Undertaker beat Koko B. Ware with the Tombstone in 6:03 at ***1/2, which was a surprise. Koko took a beating and made Taker look great."], ["p", "The Legion of Doom made their first defense against Power & Glory, Hawk pinning Hercules with the flying clothesline at 11:40 for **3/4. Animal hurt his right elbow in the match. It's not considered serious, and the word is he'll miss about one date. The problem is the next one: LOD are booked in Chicago on Saturday against the Beverly Brothers, and that card needs a change if Animal can't go. The Nasty Boys did an interview before the match calling the title change a loan, and LOD answered afterward that Jack Tunney can name the place for the rematch. That match is the obvious SummerSlam tag title match if nothing else comes along."], ["p", "Hulk Hogan beat the Warlord in 13:44 with the leg drop. It was rated -*1/2, and that's generous. The Warlord can't carry a long match and Hogan didn't try. Harvey Wippleman got involved and Sid Justice stood in the Warlord's corner for the entire match without touching anybody, which is how they want it. Sid got one line in a backstage interview, \"Look up, Hogan. I'll be there,\" and stepped on the apron after the match. Hogan pointed at the belt. No contact. Hogan's main events this month have been the weakest matches on almost every card, but the houses keep coming, and that's what the office cares about. A Hogan backstage interview was taped in Toronto and not shown on the broadcast. It's being saved as an exclusive for All American Wrestling next Sunday."], ["p", "Jim Duggan beat Sgt. Slaughter by DQ when General Adnan interfered, in 12:41 at *3/4. Twelve minutes was too long for this."], ["p", "Jake Roberts pinned Haku with the DDT at 8:28 for *3/4. The story was backstage, where Bobby Heenan told Haku to his face that he'd be with Perfect instead of at ringside for him. Haku walked off. Between the Barbarian being slow to break up the pin at Richfield, Perfect walking out on the Barbarian in Winnipeg, and now this, the Heenan Family is coming apart on camera one piece at a time. Jake is still in a holding pattern with the Warrior out."], ["p", "Dino Bravo beat Skinner with the side suplex in 7:06 at **1/4. The crowd was with Bravo, which is the whole point of putting him in Canada right now. He spoke a little French in his interview and got a good reaction."], ["p", "The Bushwhackers beat the Orient Express in 8:29 at **1/2 with Butch back from his elbow injury. They licked Sean Mooney."], ["h", "Results"], ["p", "1. Bushwhackers beat Orient Express when Luke pinned Tanaka with the battering ram in 8:29. **1/2"], ["p", "2. The Mountie beat Shane Douglas by submission (Carotid Control) in 11:03. ****1/4"], ["p", "3. Dino Bravo pinned Skinner (side suplex) in 7:06. **1/4"], ["p", "4. Shawn Michaels pinned Owen Hart (suplex) in 12:04. ***3/4"], ["p", "5. Jake Roberts pinned Haku (DDT) in 8:28. *3/4"], ["p", "6. The Undertaker pinned Koko B. Ware (Tombstone) in 6:03. ***1/2"], ["p", "7. Jim Duggan beat Sgt. Slaughter by DQ in 12:41. Adnan interfered. *3/4"], ["p", "8. IC Title: Mr. Perfect pinned SATO (Perfect-Plex) in 14:03. Heenan interfered. ****1/4"], ["p", "9. Tag Titles: LOD beat Power & Glory when Hawk pinned Hercules (flying clothesline) in 11:40. Animal hurt his elbow. **3/4"], ["p", "10. WWF Title: Hulk Hogan pinned the Warlord (leg drop) in 13:44. Wippleman interfered. -*1/2 Overall card: **"]]}, "wwf-diary-douglas-0721": {"p": "wwf", "series": "diary", "by": "Shane Douglas", "date": "1991-07-21", "place": "Toronto", "kicker": "From the Road · Toronto, July 21", "title": "Watching Heenan's Hands", "dek": "Shane Douglas loses to two champions in two days and gets Mr. Perfect again in San Francisco.", "factTitle": "The Night", "facts": [["Show", "Maple Leaf Gardens, Toronto"], ["Match", "The Mountie d. Shane Douglas"], ["Finish", "Carotid Control, 11:03"], ["Rating", "****1/4"], ["Next", "IC Title vs. Perfect, San Francisco 7/26"]], "body": [["p", "Two nights, two champions, two losses. Saturday morning the whole country watched Mr. Perfect beat me on Superstars. Tonight the Mountie choked me out in front of fourteen thousand people at the Gardens. I should feel worse than I do."], ["p", "The Mountie's a lot better than people give him credit for. He's got that cattle prod and the hat and the whole act, but in the ring he knows exactly what he's doing. We went eleven minutes and he let me have a lot of it. I had him rocked with a dropkick off the second rope. I really thought I had a chance. Then he got behind me with that Carotid Control and the lights started going out."], ["p", "I didn't quit. I faded. There's a difference, even if the referee doesn't see one."], ["p", "When I got back through the curtain, a couple of the agents were standing there. One of them said \"good match, kid,\" which in this company is like getting a raise. Owen came over too. He and Shawn tore the house down later. I watched it from the curtain. Those two are going to be wrestling each other for the next ten years."], ["p", "SATO and Perfect were the best thing on the show. Perfect beat me on Superstars, so I knew what SATO was in for. Heenan grabbed his foot, same as he does every night. Every one of us knows it's coming and none of us can stop it."], ["p", "Friday it's San Francisco, and they've given me Perfect again, this time with the belt on the line. I'll be watching Heenan's hands the whole match."], ["p", "Four days off before then. I'm going home to Pittsburgh to sleep in my own bed and ice my neck."]]}});
