Object.assign(ARTICLES, {"wwf-heenan-chooses-0721": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-21", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · Maple Leaf Gardens, Toronto, July 21", "title": "Heenan Chooses", "dek": "Bobby Heenan picked Mr. Perfect over Haku in Toronto, and Haku walked out to face Jake Roberts on his own.", "body": [["p", "Bobby Heenan made his choice backstage at Maple Leaf Gardens, and he made it to Haku's face. With Mr. Perfect defending the Intercontinental Title against SATO later in the night, Heenan told Haku he would be in the back with Perfect, not at ringside for Haku's match with Jake Roberts. Haku walked off without a word, went out alone and lost to the DDT in 8:28 (*3/4)."], ["p", "The payoff came in the semi-main, when Heenan grabbed SATO's foot on the top rope and Perfect hit the Perfect-Plex to keep the belt. The Heenan Family has carried several acts at once all summer, but this is the first time the Brain has ranked them out loud. Haku and the Barbarian already needed Barbarian to be slow to break a pin in Richfield. Whether Haku takes it quietly is the question for the Family."]], "facts": [["Date", "July 21, Maple Leaf Gardens"], ["Haku", "lost to Jake Roberts (DDT), 8:28"], ["Heenan", "backstage with Perfect"], ["IC Title", "Perfect retained vs. SATO, ****1/4"], ["Crowd", "14,200 · $255,600"]], "factTitle": "The Night"}, "wwf-sid-wont-tag-0720": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-20", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · The Spectrum, Philadelphia, July 20", "title": "Sid Won't Tag", "dek": "Sgt. Slaughter wanted the tag. Sid Justice never took it, and Harvey Wippleman wants Hogan alone at SummerSlam.", "body": [["p", "The main event at the Spectrum was Hulk Hogan & Jim Duggan against Sgt. Slaughter & Sid Justice, and Sid never set foot in the ring. Slaughter reached for the tag again and again, Sid stayed on the apron, and Hogan finished Slaughter with the Big Leg Drop at 15:23 (*1/4). Sid and Harvey Wippleman walked up the aisle before the bell, then stopped and stared Hogan down from the ramp."], ["p", "It's the clearest statement yet of where this is going. Wippleman now manages the Warlord as well as Sid, and both have spent the week around the WWF Champion: the Warlord got his title shot in Toronto the next night with Sid watching from ringside. Wippleman has been asking for Hogan vs. Sid at SummerSlam since Superstars. 15,800 paid $284,400 at the Spectrum."]], "facts": [["Match", "Hogan & Duggan d. Slaughter & Sid"], ["Finish", "Hogan pins Slaughter, Big Leg Drop, 15:23"], ["Rating", "*1/4"], ["Sid", "never tagged in"], ["Next", "Wippleman wants Hogan-Sid at SummerSlam"]], "factTitle": "The Main Event"}, "wwf-jake-and-the-urn-0720": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-20", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · The Spectrum, Philadelphia, July 20", "title": "Jake and the Urn", "dek": "Jake Roberts beat the Berzerker, stopped on the ramp, and nodded at Paul Bearer.", "body": [["p", "Jake Roberts beat the Berzerker with the DDT in 8:50 at the Spectrum (**1/4), and the match was not the story. On his way to the back Jake stopped on the ramp, looked at Paul Bearer and the urn, and nodded. Nobody said a word. Bearer held the urn up and Jake kept walking."], ["p", "This is the same Jake Roberts who took his time getting to the casket when the Undertaker went after the Ultimate Warrior at WrestleFest, and who has been beating Typhoon, Hercules and Haku since without a word about any of it. The nod is the first time he's acknowledged the Undertaker's side in public. What it means is To establish, but nobody in Philadelphia took it as a threat."]], "facts": [["Match", "Jake Roberts d. the Berzerker"], ["Finish", "DDT, 8:50"], ["Rating", "**1/4"], ["After", "Jake nods to Bearer and the urn"], ["Earlier", "late save for Warrior at WrestleFest"]], "factTitle": "The Moment"}, "wwf-bravo-walks-alone-0719": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-19", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · Richfield and Dayton, July 18–19", "title": "Bravo Walks Alone", "dek": "Jimmy Hart fired Dino Bravo in Richfield. Bravo press slammed him, then beat Hart's replacement in Dayton.", "body": [["p", "Dino Bravo lost to Tito Santana in 10:45 in Richfield on July 18, and Jimmy Hart fired him in the ring. Bravo answered by press slamming Hart over the top rope. A night later in Dayton, Hart came back with Typhoon for revenge. Bravo knocked Hart off the apron and beat Typhoon with the side suplex in 7:04, and walked out to a real cheer from the Hara Arena crowd. The match itself was -****3/4, but the reaction is what the office will remember."], ["p", "Bravo has spent most of the year as one of Jimmy Hart's heels with nothing to do. On his own he beat Skinner in Toronto on July 21. Hart still manages the Mountie and Typhoon, and he has to answer."]], "facts": [["July 18", "lost to Tito Santana, 10:45; Hart fires him"], ["After", "press slams Hart"], ["July 19", "d. Typhoon (side suplex), 7:04"], ["July 21", "d. Skinner (side suplex), 7:06"], ["Manager", "none"]], "factTitle": "Two Nights"}, "wwf-warrior-hurt-0714": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-14", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · WrestleFest '91, Busch Stadium, July 14", "title": "Warrior Hurt", "dek": "The Ultimate Warrior hurt his neck losing to the Undertaker, and Jake Roberts was in no hurry to help.", "body": [["p", "The Undertaker beat the Ultimate Warrior with the Tombstone at WrestleFest in front of 51,357 at Busch Stadium, and Warrior hurt his neck taking it. The match went -1/4*, which was the least of it. When the Undertaker and Paul Bearer went for the casket afterward, Jake Roberts came out to make the save, and took his time getting there."], ["p", "Warrior hasn't wrestled since. The neck is the reason he's off the cards; when he's back is To establish. Jake has been around the Undertaker's business ever since, and his nod to Bearer and the urn in Philadelphia on July 20 says the slow walk at WrestleFest wasn't an accident."]], "facts": [["Who", "The Ultimate Warrior"], ["Injury", "neck"], ["How", "Undertaker's Tombstone, WrestleFest 7/14"], ["Save", "Jake Roberts, late"], ["Return", "To establish"]], "factTitle": "Injury Report"}, "wwf-virgil-takes-the-belt-0719": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-19", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · Richfield and Dayton, July 18–19", "title": "Virgil Takes the Belt", "dek": "Virgil stole the Million Dollar Belt at WrestleFest, and Roddy Piper has made sure he still has it.", "body": [["p", "Virgil lost to Ted DiBiase at WrestleFest and walked out with the Million Dollar Belt anyway, with Roddy Piper running off DiBiase and IRS. Money Inc. have tried to take it back every night since. In Richfield on July 18, IRS jumped Virgil after his win over Dong Phillips and carried the belt up the ramp; Piper knocked him down and handed it back. In Dayton on July 19, IRS went for Virgil with the briefcase. Piper stopped him in front of the referee, Virgil was disqualified at 8:06, and Virgil kept the belt."], ["p", "The belt wasn't at stake in Dayton, which is why the DQ cost Virgil nothing. Piper and Virgil teamed against Money Inc. at the Spectrum on July 20, and Virgil rolled up IRS in 13:29 before DiBiase jumped him. As long as Piper is around, DiBiase isn't getting his belt back."]], "facts": [["Stolen", "WrestleFest, July 14"], ["July 18", "IRS takes it; Piper gives it back"], ["July 19", "IRS by DQ, 8:06; Virgil keeps it"], ["July 20", "Virgil & Piper d. Money Inc., 13:29"], ["Holder", "Virgil"]], "factTitle": "The Belt"}, "wwf-tito-steals-the-show-0714": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-14", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · WrestleFest '91, Busch Stadium, July 14", "title": "Tito Steals the Show", "dek": "Tito Santana's win over the Mountie was the best match at WrestleFest, and it put him in line for Mr. Perfect.", "body": [["p", "On a card with Hogan, the Undertaker and both sets of tag titles, the best match at WrestleFest was Tito Santana and the Mountie in the middle of the show. Tito won with the flying forearm (***3/4). The Mountie took it out on the Big Boss Man with the shock stick afterward, which set up its own feud."], ["p", "Tito hasn't slowed down since. He beat Dino Bravo in Richfield, the Warlord on Superstars and Rick Martel at the Spectrum (****1/4), and he's the obvious next challenger for Mr. Perfect's Intercontinental Title. Heenan said on Superstars that Perfect will defend against anybody at SummerSlam. Tito is the anybody."]], "facts": [["Match", "Tito Santana d. the Mountie"], ["Finish", "flying forearm"], ["Rating", "***3/4, match of the night"], ["After", "Mountie shocks Boss Man"], ["Next", "in line for Mr. Perfect"]], "factTitle": "The Match"}, "wwf-crush-vs-tenryu-0714": {"p": "wwf", "news": 1, "by": "Newsletter staff", "date": "1991-07-14", "region": "WWF Report", "kicker": "WWF · Milwaukee and WrestleFest, July 13–14", "title": "Crush vs. Tenryu", "dek": "Crush and Genichiro Tenryu have fought twice in two nights, and a singles match is coming to Superstars.", "body": [["p", "Crush pinned Genichiro Tenryu with the backbreaker at the Bradley Center in Milwaukee on July 13 (*1/2), one of the SWS stars on loan to the WWF this summer. Tenryu got a measure of revenge at WrestleFest, sort of: with Crush at ringside for Demolition against Tenryu & Koji Kitao, Crush pulled Tenryu out of the ring so Smash could pin Kitao with the backbreaker. The two of them kept brawling after the bell."], ["p", "A Crush vs. Tenryu singles match is coming to Superstars. Tenryu is a top name in Japan and the WWF has been careful with him. Losing clean to Crush in a house show wasn't careful. A televised rematch is the chance to fix that."]], "facts": [["July 13", "Crush d. Tenryu (backbreaker), *1/2"], ["July 14", "Demolition d. Tenryu & Kitao"], ["Crush", "pulled Tenryu out; brawl after"], ["Next", "Crush vs. Tenryu on Superstars"], ["Tenryu", "SWS"]], "factTitle": "The Feud"}});
